window.BC=window.BC||{};window.BC.meta={"CAISO": {"groups": ["COAL", "COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "groupLabel": {"COAL": "Coal", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil"}, "zones": ["NP15", "SP15", "ZP26"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, "ERCOT": {"groups": ["COAL_LIGNITE", "COAL_PRB", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "groupLabel": {"COAL_LIGNITE": "Coal Lignite", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil"}, "zones": ["Houston", "North", "Northeast", "South", "South_Central", "West"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, "MISO": {"groups": ["COAL_LIGNITE", "COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "groupLabel": {"COAL_LIGNITE": "Coal Lignite", "COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil"}, "zones": ["MISO-Central", "MISO-North", "MISO-South"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, "NEISO": {"groups": ["COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "groupLabel": {"COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil"}, "zones": ["Boston", "Central", "Connecticut", "North"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, "NYISO": {"groups": ["COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP"], "groupLabel": {"COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP"}, "zones": ["Capital_Hudson", "Long_Island", "NYC", "Upstate_West"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}, "PJM": {"groups": ["COAL_PRB", "COAL_BIT", "COAL_WC", "CC_REGULAR", "CC_CHP", "CT_PEAKER", "CT_CHP", "ST_GAS", "ST_CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL"], "groupLabel": {"COAL_PRB": "Coal PRB", "COAL_BIT": "Coal Bituminous", "COAL_WC": "Coal Waste", "CC_REGULAR": "CC Regular", "CC_CHP": "CC CHP", "CT_PEAKER": "CT Peaker", "CT_CHP": "CT CHP", "ST_GAS": "Steam Gas", "ST_CHP": "Steam CHP", "OTHER_FOSSIL": "Other Fossil"}, "zones": ["PJM_AEP_Ohio", "PJM_ATSI", "PJM_Central_PA", "PJM_ComEd", "PJM_Dominion", "PJM_EMAAC", "PJM_SWMAAC", "PJM_West_APS"], "years": [2023, 2024, 2025]}};window.BC.manifest=[{"id": "2026-06-18-neiso-19-ct-mustrun", "label": "neiso 19 ct mustrun", "date": "2026-06-18", "shorthand": "neiso-19-ct-mustrun", "definition": "neiso 19 (PROBE \u2014 rejected for the 2025 CC gap; informative on the CT wedge). Baseline (neiso 17) + --ct-mustrun-per-plant: forces each CT_PEAKER to its observed EIA-923 monthly net generation (the full reliability/reserve wedge, WEFOR/POF-exempt), vs baseline's sub-marginal --ct-deployment slice. CT_PEAKER TWh (model / EIA-923 %): 0.350 -15% / 0.356 -45% / 0.270 -46% (2023/24/25) vs baseline 0.084/0.052/0.079 (-80/-93/-84%) \u2014 recovers the wedge (2025 0.08->0.27; capped below 0.50 actual because the 2025 early-release 923 lists only 247 CT plants). Answers the candidate-B question: the reserve wedge (observed-energy floor) IS larger than the sub-marginal slice (cf. neiso 20 ct-deploy2x at 0.15 TWh). BUT CC_REGULAR barely moves: +8.7%->+8.4% in 2025 (-0.18 TWh, ~1:1 with the CT added) \u2014 the CT wedge is real but ~0.4 TWh, far too small to close the +4.6 TWh CC over-dispatch. ST_GAS (-95%) untouched (CT-only lever). 2023/24 CC stay in tol (-0.6/-0.7%). Verdict: correct physics, wrong magnitude for the headline gap; keep as the CT-class fix, not the CC fix.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-18-neiso-19-ct-mustrun.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-18-neiso-20-ct-deploy2x", "label": "neiso 20 ct deploy2x", "date": "2026-06-18", "shorthand": "neiso-20-ct-deploy2x", "definition": "neiso 20 (PROBE \u2014 rejected; negative control for the CT wedge). Baseline (neiso 17) + --ct-deployment-floor-frac 2.0: doubles the measured sub-marginal CT AS/RUC-deployment floor (0.08->0.15 TWh of forced energy). CT_PEAKER TWh (model / EIA-923 %): 0.113 -73% / 0.066 -90% / 0.096 -81% (2023/24/25) vs baseline 0.084/0.052/0.079 \u2014 barely moves (the sub-marginal slice is nearly exhausted at frac 1.0). CC_REGULAR unchanged at +8.7% in 2025 (-0.2/-0.3% in 2023/24, no regression, no improvement). Confirms the candidate-B contrast: the observed-energy reserve wedge (neiso 19 ct-mustrun, CT 0.27-0.36) is ~2-3x the sub-marginal deployment slice; scaling the slice is a dead end. Does nothing for the headline CC over-dispatch.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-18-neiso-20-ct-deploy2x.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-19-neiso-21-monthly-keeper", "label": "neiso 21 monthly keeper", "date": "2026-06-19", "shorthand": "neiso-21-monthly-keeper", "definition": "neiso 21 (KEEPER \u2014 measured monthly hub-basis overlay; no fitted daily-AGT curve). Base: --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly. CC_REGULAR (model / 923 %): 52.31 -0.1% / 56.48 -0.2% / 57.75 +8.7% (2023/24/25); 2023/24 in tolerance. RE-ANCHORED SCORING \u2014 the 2025 +8.7% is NOT a 4.6 TWh model error: (1) the gas envelope is correct \u2014 model gas 60.14 ~= EIA-930 60.09 (+0.1%), net_gen 107.28 ~= 107.15; (2) CAMPD/CEMS triangulation on the common CC plant set gives model/CAMPD = +16.7/+13.8/+19.0% (2023/24/25) and EIA-923/CAMPD = +17.9/+14.9/+10.4%. The 923/CAMPD ratio holds a stable ~+15-18% in 2023/24 but COLLAPSES to +10.4% in 2025 => the preliminary EIA-923 CC_2025 is ~2.2 TWh undercounted (still filling in), not ground truth. Decompose the +8.7% (~4.6 TWh over 923): ~2.2 TWh is 923 incompleteness, ~2 TWh is a small within-gas over-dispatch (model +19% vs its ~+15% CAMPD norm). Real CC error ~+3-4%, NOT +8.7%; the residual %-gap is benchmark incompleteness, not model error \u2014 do not tune CC down to the 53.1 preliminary-923 bar. The fitted --gas-hub-basis-daily daily-AGT overlay was REJECTED this session: its convexity is fitted to the backcast and tuning it to hit EIA-930 oil is curve-fitting a magic number, so the keeper stays on the measured monthly overlay.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-19-neiso-21-monthly-keeper.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-19-neiso-22-ccsteam-keeper", "label": "neiso 22 ccsteam keeper", "date": "2026-06-19", "shorthand": "neiso-22-ccsteam-keeper", "definition": "neiso 22 (KEEPER \u2014 supersedes neiso 21). Re-ran the monthly-keeper config (--commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly) to integrate the combined-cycle steam-turbine unit-outage derate (eia_*_cc capacity coupling in campd-unit-outages-NEISO.csv; derivation-script level, NO magic numbers \u2014 the fitted daily-AGT convexity stays rejected), and fixed a poisoned gas-basis cell. VERDICT: NEISO is backcast-calibrated. CC_REGULAR 52.30/56.51/57.76 reproduces the keeper (52.31/56.48/57.75) within +/-0.03 TWh; gas within ~1% of EIA-930 all years (54.76/58.87/60.11 vs 55.47/59.64/60.09); LW hub $35.08/$40.52/$71.20 vs actual DA $36.82/$41.47/$67.86 (-4.7/-2.3/+4.9%); the 2025 monthly DA shape tracks actual within a few $/MWh every month. The CC-steam derate is a NEAR-NO-OP for NEISO \u2014 controlled A/B (only the outage CSV swapped) moves 2025 CC_REGULAR -0.02 TWh and LW price +$0.9; the fleet has the headroom to absorb it (adopt for cross-ISO consistency). SEPARATELY FIXED (commit aa62655): the 15:46 fetch-eia-gas-prices refresh (postdates neiso 21) wrote a +$13.46/MMBtu Aug-2025 AGT basis from the EIA MA citygate proxy \u2014 a winter-level summer-month LDC citygate average that drove modeled Aug-2025 LMP to $167 vs the actual $45.6 DA; replaced with the Jul/Sep interpolation (+0.04). Before a forecast run: the neighbor-convexity priced-import node is REQUIRED (the seam is a fixed measured schedule today), plus the derived daily-AGT winter-oil convexity when upload U4 lands (oil 0.00/0.00/0.06 vs EIA-930 0.32/0.37/1.24, LMP-neutral). Full diagnosis: results/calibration/DIAGNOSIS-neiso-ccsteam-2026-06-19.md; forecast-prep prompts: docs/multi-iso/neiso-forecast-prep-prompts.md. SYSTEM VOLUME ERROR FIX (render_calibration_html, incomplete-vintage benchmark repair): the 2025 fossil benchmark was raw preliminary EIA-923, under-counting CC by ~4 TWh (CC bench 53.12 vs model 57.76, which matches complete EIA-930 within 1%), inflating the 2025 system volume error to +7.25%. The benchmark now scales any fossil fuel whose grid 923 total is <0.97x the complete EIA-930 grid series up to that EIA-930 total (split/shape preserved); 2025 sys vol err +7.25% -> ~0%, complete vintages (2023 1.02x / 2024 1.02x) untouched. CEMS audit confirmed CEMS under-reads CC every year (44-49 vs 923's 52-57) so it is a shape source, not a level authority; the model is correct.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-19-neiso-22-ccsteam-keeper.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-19-pjm-33-miso-reanchor", "label": "pjm 33 miso reanchor", "date": "2026-06-19", "shorthand": "pjm-33-miso-reanchor", "definition": "Forecast-grade reference-price interchange seam (pjm_32 lineage) with the dominant MISO seam now anchored entirely to MISO's OWN measured Indiana-Hub RT LMP (fetched from docs.misoenergy.org), replacing estimates (claude.md #12): marginal heat rate 14.2->12.9 (14.2 used the IMM ~$36 all-footprint average; the border Indiana Hub measures $31.8/$30.8/$42.85 = LMP/HH 12.5/14.1/12.2, mean 12.9 -> 14.2 over-priced the border by +13%/+17% in 2023/2025) and convexity 1.63->1.60 (self-derived, R2 0.24 -- confirms the borrow was sound, so convexity was NOT the over-export driver; the level was). derive_neighbor_convexity --check now guards both. RESULT vs pjm_32: net export corrects toward measured every year -- 2024 +34%->+1.8% (33.3 vs 32.7 TWh, near-perfect), 2025 +112%->+30% (23.4 vs 18.0), 2023 +56%->+32% (52.7 vs 40.0); in-tol fails 7->5; 2024 now fully passes (coal-tot +1.0%, gas +4.8%). HONEST TRADE-OFF: with interchange correctly sized the LMP under-run is no longer masked by over-export -- 2025 LMP -13.7% (was pjm_32 -10.5%, pjm_28 -16.8%), 2024 -6.4%, 2023 +2.1% -- revealing the residual under-run is PJM's OWN price-formation gap (scarcity/congestion/reserve adders the energy-only LP omits), NOT interchange. Residual coal-over in 2023/2025 (+7%) is the forecast-native single MISO HR (12.9) slightly over-pricing those years (their measured ratios 12.5/12.2 < 12.9); a per-year HR would close it but isn't forecastable. Most structurally faithful PJM run to date (every seam parameter measured from the neighbor's own data, nothing tuned to PJM flow, rule #11); pjm_28 remains the lowest-fail FITTED reference.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-19-pjm-33-miso-reanchor.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-19-pjm-34-coal-rank", "label": "pjm 34 coal rank", "date": "2026-06-19", "shorthand": "pjm-34-coal-rank", "definition": "pjm_33 lineage (forecast-grade reference-price interchange seam, MISO reanchor) + a CORRECTNESS fix to coal-rank labeling: three retiring PJM coal plants \u2014 W H Sammis (OH), Homer City (PA), AES Warrior Run (MD), all bituminous \u2014 were stuck in the model's generic unranked COAL bucket because coal_supply_class consulted only the curated + EIA-923 fuel-receipt maps, and a mid-backcast retiree has no recent burned-fuel receipts to derive a rank from. The EIA-923 benchmark already resolves them to COAL_BIT via fuel code, so the model showed a spurious generic 'Coal' row the actual never has AND priced them at generic full cost instead of PJM's bituminous passthrough. FIX (data.fleet.coal_supply_class): a tertiary EIA-860 energy_source fallback scoped to the retired-within-window vintage (the exact structural gap; operable coal untouched), mirroring the benchmark's fuel-code fallback. PJM coal now fully resolves (32 bituminous, 0 unranked); 212 fleet/coal tests pass. KEY FINDING \u2014 the dashboard's '+7%' 2023 coal-over was a LABELING ARTIFACT: the coal-tot gate sums only BIT+PRB+WC, so it compared model-coal-MINUS-the-3-retirees (~13 TWh hidden in the excluded generic bucket) against actual-coal-that-INCLUDES-them. Apples-to-apples the 2023 coal-over is +18.7% (+21 TWh), not +7%; total model coal barely moved (136.7->134.8 TWh, repricing ~dispatch-neutral) \u2014 only the attribution became honest. SCORECARD vs actual (coal-tot %; LMP %): 2023 coal-tot +18.7% (was +6.9%), COAL_BIT +20.0%, gas +2.2%, LMP +2.4%, net-export +29.6% | 2024 coal-tot +3.9% (was +1.0%, still PASS, 0 fails), LMP -6.3% | 2025 coal-tot +7.5% (UNCHANGED from pjm_33 \u2014 no retirees operating, so this is the GENUINE coal-over, not labeling), LMP -13.7%. in-tol fails 5 (same count as pjm_33; 2023 COAL_BIT/coal-tot, 2025 COAL_BIT/COAL_PRB/coal-tot). ROOT CAUSE now visible: ~half the 2023 coal-over is the 3 retirees over-dispatching (Homer City model 9.1 vs 1.0 TWh actual) \u2014 part a COD-ramp partial-retirement bug (load_cod_map collapses heterogeneous unit-retirement dates to the latest, so Homer City runs 2 units that retired Jul/Aug through year-end; small-scope 4-plant cross-ISO fix deferred), part the dominant economic over-dispatch of cheap uneconomic retirees the energy-only LP runs as baseload (needs CAMPD unit-level availability, not a fit). LMP/interchange unchanged vs pjm_33; this run makes the coal scorecard HONEST (faithfulness over MAE) and supersedes pjm_33's misleading coal-over.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-19-pjm-34-coal-rank.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-19-pjm-35-cc-steam", "label": "pjm 35 cc-steam rebaseline", "date": "2026-06-19", "shorthand": "pjm-35-cc-steam", "definition": "KEEPER. Re-baseline of the pjm_34 recipe (pjm_28 config + forecast-grade reference-price interchange seam + neighbor price-vs-load convexity) on the CC steam-turbine outage coupling now in main: a CC's CT outage derates its full block (CT + the steam it fed) instead of the CT alone, so the steam is no longer phantom-available during CT outages. Nothing tuned to PJM's net-MWh/price residual. Coal-tot over: 2023 +15.7% (was pjm_34 +18.7%), 2024 +5.0% (+3.9%), 2025 +8.7% (+7.5%); gas in tolerance all years (+1.4/+3.6/+3.0%). FINDING: the bituminous 0.76 passthrough floor is NOT the coal-over lever \u2014 2025 has zero retirees yet COAL_BIT overs +7.8% at a ~1.08 passthrough (near full fuel cost, $3.52 gas ~ gas_mid 3.40), and the over runs INVERSE to the cheap-gas discount (2024 deepest discount 0.79 -> smallest over). Residual coal-over is structural (operable-coal baseload + 2023/24 retiree over-dispatch: Homer City/Sammis/Warrior Run 6.25 TWh in 2023, 3.2 in 2024, 0 in 2025), addressable only by measured COD fractional-retirement + CAMPD unit availability, not by tuning the passthrough.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-19-pjm-35-cc-steam.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-20-pjm-36-retiree-cems", "label": "pjm 36 retiree cems", "date": "2026-06-20", "shorthand": "pjm-36-retiree-cems", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes pjm 35 cc-steam). pjm_35 config + within-window retiree CEMS measured-availability cap (retiree_cems_cap=True): each within-window retiree plant is limited to its measured monthly CAMPD CEMS envelope (zero in months it did not run), honestly reflecting the out-of-market retirement economics a cost-based LP cannot see \u2014 nothing tuned to the coal/net-MWh target. Closes the Homer City (3122) phantom-baseload over: model 4.40/2.79/0.00 \u2192 1.48/0.00/0.00 TWh vs CEMS 1.20/0.00/0.00. Two compounding fixes: (a) per-unit COD retirement in cod_ramp (effective_cod no longer collapses heterogeneous unit retirements to the latest date) \u2014 MAE-neutral alone, the per-plant binning merges units before the ramp; (b) the CEMS cap, which moves the aggregate. Coal-tot: 2023 +15.7%\u2192+13.8%, 2024 +5.0%\u2192+3.1% (now PASS), 2025 +8.7% unchanged (no running retirees \u2014 clean blast radius). In-tolerance fails 7\u21925; freed retiree energy split between gas (stays PASS) and reduced 2023 over-export (interchg +15.0%\u2192+11.6%). Finding: the residual operable-coal over (2025 +7.8% BIT, confound-free) is system merit-order, NOT a retiree-capacity issue.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-20-pjm-36-retiree-cems.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-21-neiso-23-outage-btm", "label": "neiso 23 outage-btm-basis", "date": "2026-06-21", "shorthand": "neiso-23-outage-btm", "definition": "neiso 23 (KEEPER \u2014 supersedes neiso 22). Two folded, independently-attributable changes re-solved in one byte-faithful pass off the neiso-22 run_config (scripts/run_calibration_full.py --iso NEISO --year 2023 2024 2025 --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; gas-hub-basis monthly overlay on by default). TASK A \u2014 OUTAGE RE-GATE (model-side): swapped in the net-load-filtered (revealed-availability) data/raw/campd-unit-outages-NEISO.csv (branch claude/ercot-scarcity-tuning-handoff-nl1y3m @ b0c41cb), capacity-weighted outage GW-days 6150->4049 (-34%; summer binding preserved, shoulder/winter economic-idle mass cut). Everything else identical -> the dispatch/system/storage parquets are the pure outage delta. Near-no-op on dispatch, as expected from the keeper's headroom: CC_REGULAR 52.34/56.54/57.89 TWh (keeper 52.31/56.48/57.75; +0.03/+0.06/+0.14). Cooler shoulder/winter LMP, the expected revealed-availability direction: load-wtd hub $34.06/$39.00/$69.60 (keeper $35.08/$40.52/$71.20; vs actual DA $36.82/$41.47/$67.86 -> -7.5%/-6.0%/+2.6%, was -4.7/-2.3/+4.9%). FLAG: 2023/24 now run ~6-7.5% below actual DA (a ~$1-1.5 cooling) \u2014 modest, not an under-fire; summer/tail preserved (zone-avg hrs>$200: 0/0/13). TASK B \u2014 CHP BTM BASIS FIX (benchmark-side; solve-invariant): the grid LP holds out behind-the-meter CHP host self-supply for ALL ISOs, so gmModel is grid-delivered only, but btm.parquet was written ERCOT-only (run_calibration_full.py gated btm_frames.append on is_ercot). NEISO render fell back to FULL EIA-923 (BTM included) vs a grid-only model -> a false ~1.3 TWh/yr CHP shortfall (~60% of the prior -3.8/-4.1% 2023/24 system volume error). FIX: removed the is_ercot gate so _btm_frame runs for every ISO, building the per-plant CHP bin frame from the year's EIA-860 fleet group map (the non-ERCOT analogue of the curated CAMPD bin sheet) instead of the ERCOT-only sheet; plant codes parsed numeric-head like the dispatch frame. btm.parquet CHP btm_twh now present: CC_CHP 0.58/0.66/0.52, CT_CHP 0.56/0.53/0.13, ST_CHP ~0 (model slightly over-produces ST_CHP, so 923-grid clips to 0 \u2014 within abs band, not chased). classFull = EIA-923 - per-class BTM now lands on gmModel: CC_CHP 1.35/1.36/1.48 vs model 1.36/1.35/1.44 (|d|<=0.04, in tolerance all 3 yrs); CT_CHP (gate-excluded) 1.04/1.03/0.33, within +/-1 TWh abs. SYSTEM GAS VOLUME ERROR 2023 -1.3% / 2024 -1.6% / 2025 +0.4% (down from -3.8/-4.1% on the false full-923 basis; CC_REGULAR -0.04%/-0.13%/+0.75% vs classFull). 2025 stays ~0% anchored by the EIA-930 incomplete-vintage reconcile (render _VINTAGE_RECONCILE_FRAC=0.97; gas scaled x1.082, complete 2023/24 untouched at 1.001/1.005); the uniform reconcile re-inflates 2025 CC_CHP classFull 1.36->1.48 but model 1.44 still sits inside tolerance, so no CC_REGULAR-concentration override was needed. Fixing at the source (btm.parquet) keeps render, session_score, compare_runs_classes and the offer-curve Jacobian on the same grid-delivered basis. The remaining ~0.8 TWh of the 2023 system gap is small peaker/steam classes the model zeroes (CT_PEAKER, ST_GAS, COAL_BIT) \u2014 real tiny dispatch differences within +/-1 TWh abs, NOT a reporting artifact; not conflated with the BTM basis. FOLLOW-UP (out of scope here): the same BTM gate fix will correctly affect the PJM/CAISO/NYISO keepers once their bundles regenerate btm.parquet \u2014 re-register those later. Carries forward all neiso-22 forecast-prep caveats (priced-import seam node REQUIRED before a forecast run; daily-AGT winter-oil convexity stays rejected as curve-fitting). Full neiso-22 diagnosis: results/calibration/DIAGNOSIS-neiso-ccsteam-2026-06-19.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-21-neiso-23-outage-btm.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-21-pjm-38-outage-regate", "label": "pjm 38 outage regate", "date": "2026-06-21", "shorthand": "pjm-38-outage-regate", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes pjm 36 retiree-cems). The pjm 36 keeper config (retiree_cems_cap=True) re-solved byte-faithfully on the net-load-filtered outages (commit b0c41cb), changing ONLY the outage input \u2014 the cross-ISO revealed-availability filter that cut PJM capacity-weighted outage GW-days ~46% (concentrated Apr/Oct, summer preserved). Promoted under right-structure-first: the net-load filter is the forecast-defensible exogenous outage input (keys on net load, not price). Honest gate result (NOT clean \u2014 accepted as forecast-native, see docs/multi-iso/pjm-outage-regate-2026-06.md): the cooler shoulder/winter availability lets the cost-based LP run the freed cheap take-or-pay coal/CC, amplifying PJM's documented forecast-native coal over-run + over-export. Coal-tot resid 2023 +6.8->+17.3%, 2024 -2.2->+13.9%, 2025 +2.4->+22.8%; net-export +11.6->+69% / -11.8->+65% / +28->+204%; in-tolerance fails 3->11. LMP cools further below actual (load-wtd mean 30.09->28.12 / 28.06->26.17 / 37.81->34.63; lwt-MAE 8.30->8.12 / 10.46->10.49 / 15.06->15.82; hrs>$200 0/0/0 unchanged). The coal-over/export is the next frontier (offer-curve / coal-cost retune), not chased here. | BTM RE-SCORE / FIRST REGISTRATION (2026-06-22): pjm_38 was a stub (no payload); re-solved its committed keeper config from meta.json, writing btm.parquet + full payload + bench. C6 governance PASS (attestation). DETERMINATION: NOT-YET. Deciding fails are MODEL MISSes (not ledgerable): C2 coal +17.3/+13.9/+22.8% (the documented PJM structural coal over-run, a SEPARATE issue amplified by the net-load re-gate, not folded in here), CT_PEAKER -18/-33% under, CC_CHP +1-2 over measured 923 grid, C3a LMP -11.4/-19.3% (2024/25). C4 PASS.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-21-pjm-38-outage-regate.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-miso1-baseline", "label": "miso1 baseline", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "miso1-baseline", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle miso1_baseline.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-miso1-baseline.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-neiso-24-local-band", "label": "neiso 24 local band", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "neiso-24-local-band", "definition": "neiso 24 local-band outage regate (NEW KEEPER, supersedes neiso 23). The neiso-23 keeper config (scripts/run_calibration_full.py --iso NEISO --year 2023 2024 2025 --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; gas-hub-basis monthly overlay on by default; CHP BTM basis fix in code) re-solved BYTE-FAITHFULLY on the LOCAL (seasonal) net-load outage band \u2014 high_load_mask now uses a centered rolling +/-30d window (commit 152bb99) instead of a single annual percentile, recovering genuine multi-week shoulder maintenance outages. ONLY the outage input changed vs neiso 23 (regenerated data/raw/campd-unit-outages-NEISO.csv, auto picked up). NEAR-NO-OP as predicted by the keeper's dispatch headroom (neiso-23 noted the same for the annual-band re-gate): CC_REGULAR 52.32/56.48/57.75 TWh (neiso-23 52.34/56.54/57.89; |d|<=0.14); system gas volume error -1.5%/-1.4%/+0.2% (neiso-23 -1.3/-1.6/+0.4). LMP demand-wtd model mean 33.93/39.23/68.33 (neiso-23 34.06/39.00/69.60; vs actual DA 36.82/41.47/67.86 -> -7.8%/-5.4%/+0.7%, marginally closer to actual on 2024/2025, ~flat 2023). Hourly hub MAE 15.23/14.11/21.60. TAIL PRESERVED: model hrs>$200 0/0/13 (IDENTICAL to neiso-23; actual 72/43/290) \u2014 summer/winter scarcity neither collapsed nor re-inflated (2025 model max $258.98). The local band is the forecast-defensible exogenous outage input (keys on net load, not price) and produces an equivalent-or-marginally-better calibration; adopted as the keeper-of-record on the correct input. Carries forward all neiso-23/neiso-22 caveats (priced-import seam node required before a forecast run; daily-AGT winter-oil convexity stays rejected).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-neiso-24-local-band.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-neiso-25-fullstop-override", "label": "neiso 25 fullstop override", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "neiso-25-fullstop-override", "definition": "neiso 25 fullstop-override outage re-gate (NEW KEEPER, supersedes neiso 24). The neiso-24 keeper config (run_calibration_full.py --iso NEISO --year 2023 2024 2025 --commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; monthly gas-hub-basis overlay on by default; CHP BTM basis fix in code) re-solved on the SHARED FULL-STOP DURATION OVERRIDE outage input (commits 56ca468/debad10): filter_revealed_outages now keeps a detected down-span if it overlaps >=24 high-net-load hours OR is a sustained full stop (span mean-CF<0.02) for >=14 continuous days, recovering the thin layer of genuinely-mechanical 14-20d weeks-long dead stops the local band still dropped at the 24h overlap margin. Measured-only (CAMPD CF + EIA-930 net load), one shared N=14/depth across all ISOs, no per-ISO constant, no price/MWh-residual (claude.md #11/#12: keep real measured outages). NEISO recovered +39 windows / 15 spans (Merrimack coal, Granite Ridge/Newington/Westbrook/Bellingham CC, Stony Brook, Pittsfield), 0.6% of detected GW-days, all small units; the STEP-1 audit (docs/multi-iso/outage-gate-fullstop-override-2026-06.md) predicted a near-no-op for NEISO and it is. ONLY the outage CSV changed vs neiso 24 (auto picked up). RESULT vs neiso 24: gas tot 54.82/58.92/60.32 TWh vs EIA-930 55.47/59.64/60.09 -> -1.2%/-1.2%/+0.4% (neiso-24 -1.5/-1.4/+0.2, marginally CLOSER to EIA-930 on 2023/24); CC_REGULAR 52.35/56.50/57.88 (neiso-24 52.32/56.48/57.75; |d|<=0.13). LMP model mean 33.96/39.24/68.74 (neiso-24 33.93/39.23/68.33) vs actual DA 36.82/41.51/67.86 -> -7.8%/-5.5%/+1.3%. Hourly hub MAE vs actual RT 15.24/14.10/21.64 (IDENTICAL to neiso-24 15.23/14.11/21.60). TAIL PRESERVED: model hrs>$200 0/0/14 (neiso-24 0/0/13; actual RT 72/43/290), 2025 model max $258.98 unchanged -- the recovered winter full-stops (Granite Ridge/Merrimack Feb 2025) add exactly one marginal 2025 scarcity hour, neither collapsing nor re-inflating the tail. The full-stop override is the structurally-correct forecast-defensible exogenous outage input (keys on net load + measured CF, regenerates forward) and produces an equivalent-or-marginally-better calibration; adopted as keeper-of-record on the correct input. Carries forward all neiso-24/23/22 caveats: priced-import seam node REQUIRED before a forecast run (imports on a fixed EIA-930 schedule); winter oil under-generation (model 0.00/0.00/0.06 vs EIA-930 0.32/0.37/1.24 TWh) is the monthly-AGT-granularity limit and the dominant remaining residual -- the >$200 winter tail (0/0/14 vs 72/43/290) is downstream of it; daily-AGT winter-oil convexity stays REJECTED as a fitted constant (derive from measured daily AGT spot, upload U4).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-neiso-25-fullstop-override.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-nyiso-18-local-floors", "label": "nyiso 18 local floors", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "nyiso-18-local-floors", "definition": "(PROBE) Long Island local self-supply floor (NYISO LMIC, frac 0.45) + HQ/Ontario firm-import baseload. STRUCTURAL fix: LI in-zone generation 3.7->9.3 TWh (real 8.5) \u2014 the -4.8 TWh dispatch error is fixed and the LI dual-fuel units fire. Price MAE 8.2/4.0/10.9 (keeper ~8.0/4.3/6.0): 2024 better, but the floor adds cheap forced supply on top of the already-missing scarcity tail and worsens 2025 (Jun/Jul -22..-31). Per rule #1 the structurally-correct floor stays in code (default OFF); it becomes a keeper once the scarcity tail (fix #3 / in-LP reserve co-optimization for NYC peakers) lands. See docs/nyiso-dispatch-validation-2026-06.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-nyiso-18-local-floors.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-nyiso-19-locational-reserve", "label": "nyiso 19 locational reserve", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "nyiso-19-locational-reserve", "definition": "(PROBE) Fix #3: in-LP LOCATIONAL reserve co-optimization for NYISO (NYCA-system plus nested East/SENY/NYC RCPF families, FERC ER21-502 / RS4 anchors), on top of the LI self-supply + firm-import floors (--energy-reserve-coopt --nyiso-local-selfsupply --nyiso-firm-imports). Holding the import-constrained downstate pocket to its locational reserve recovers the summer scarcity tail the NYCA-aggregate curve and the post-solve RCPF adder cannot dispatch: reserve fires only in genuine summer scarcity (Jul/Aug/Sep), 2025 summer under-bias eased (Jun -30.7->-14.6, Jul -22.1->-15.8 vs the LI-floor probe), 2025 price_shape now PASSES (NRMSE 0.16) and 2024 price fully PASSES, dispatch r 0.81-0.87 all years. NOT a keeper: the aggregate is gated by a PRE-EXISTING winter/January downstate over-pricing (2023 price_mean +13%, shape 0.402) that a BASE-vs-COOPT A/B proved is identical with the co-opt off (a gas/dual-fuel marginal-unit residual, the next root cause) -- so it does not beat keeper nyiso-11. Per rule #1 the mechanism stays (default OFF).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-nyiso-19-locational-reserve.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-pjm-39-local-band", "label": "pjm 39 local band", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "pjm-39-local-band", "definition": "pjm 39 local-band outage regate (NEW KEEPER, supersedes pjm 38). The pjm-38 keeper config (retiree_cems_cap=True) re-solved BYTE-FAITHFULLY on the LOCAL (seasonal) net-load outage band \u2014 scripts/derive_campd_outages.py high_load_mask now measures the high-load percentile over a centered rolling +/-30d window (commit 152bb99) instead of a single annual percentile, recovering genuine multi-week shoulder CCGT maintenance outages the annual band wrongly dropped while still dropping short economic-idle gaps. ONLY the outage input changed vs pjm 38 (both regenerated data/raw/campd-outages-PJM.csv facility + campd-unit-outages-PJM.csv unit, auto picked up); no offers/curves/flags retuned. Direction as predicted: recovering shoulder outages puts MORE coal/CC offline in shoulder months -> firmer prices and LESS of the annual-band coal over-run/over-export. STRICT IMPROVEMENT over pjm 38 on every axis. LMP demand-wtd mean (model vs actual RT) 28.29/26.12/35.01 (pjm38 28.12/26.17/34.63; actual 28.44/29.53/42.89); hourly hub MAE 7.94/9.75/13.37 (pjm38 8.12/10.49/15.82 \u2014 better all 3 yrs); coal-tot resid +11.4/+3.0/+11.1% (pjm38 +17.3/+13.9/+22.8); net-export resid +31/+17.5/+93% (pjm38 +69/+65/+204); in-tolerance fails 11->7. Model hrs>$200 0/0/0 unchanged (actual 6/18/59) \u2014 the model still does not fire PJM's scarcity tail, a documented SEPARATE issue, neither collapsed nor re-inflated here. Summer not under-fired (2023 Jul/Aug model 29.1 vs actual 31.8). Remaining coal over-run + over-export is PJM's documented forecast-native coal-cost/offer issue (docs/multi-iso/pjm-coal-mustrun-floor.md), made LESS visible than the annual band rather than newly introduced \u2014 the next frontier, not chased in the outage step. [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-pjm-39-local-band.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-pjm-40-coal-fidelity", "label": "pjm 40 coal fidelity", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "pjm-40-coal-fidelity", "definition": "pjm_40_coalfidelity: pjm_39 local-band config (verbatim) + the heat-input->MWh proxy for grossLoad-blank coal + the coal-cogen CHP/BTM holdout (Eastman/St Nicholas/John B Rich/...). Measured-anchored, no new flags; 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-pjm-40-coal-fidelity.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-pjm-41-fullstop-override", "label": "pjm 41 fullstop override", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "pjm-41-fullstop-override", "definition": "pjm_40_coalfidelity: pjm_39 local-band config (verbatim) + the heat-input->MWh proxy for grossLoad-blank coal + the coal-cogen CHP/BTM holdout (Eastman/St Nicholas/John B Rich/...). Measured-anchored, no new flags; 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-pjm-41-fullstop-override.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-pjm-42-campd-nan", "label": "pjm 42 campd nan", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "pjm-42-campd-nan", "definition": "pjm_37_retiree: pjm_36 refconvex config + within-window retiree CEMS measured-availability cap (retiree_cems_cap=True) \u2014 each retiree plant limited to its measured monthly CAMPD envelope, closing the Homer City phantom-baseload over without tuning to the coal target, 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-pjm-42-campd-nan.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-run146-zonal-gas-basis", "label": "run146 zonal gas basis", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "run146-zonal-gas-basis", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-run146-zonal-gas-basis.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-run147-new-outage-baseline", "label": "run147 new outage baseline", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "run147-new-outage-baseline", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-run147-new-outage-baseline.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-run147-zonal-gas-ep", "label": "run147 zonal gas ep", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "run147-zonal-gas-ep", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-run147-zonal-gas-ep.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-run149-zonal-gas-prb", "label": "run149 zonal gas prb", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "run149-zonal-gas-prb", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-run149-zonal-gas-prb.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-22-run150-stgas-netload-drag", "label": "run150 stgas netload drag", "date": "2026-06-22", "shorthand": "run150-stgas-netload-drag", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-22-run150-stgas-netload-drag.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-23-bidir-basis", "label": "caiso 23 bidir+basis-only", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-23-bidir-basis", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 rejected, keeper stays caiso-import-cap-floor) DECOMPOSITION of the rejected caiso-21 unify: isolate the per-tranche delivered-cost BASIS on the bidir-intertie import legs ALONE, with the RA gas-commitment floor explicitly OFF (--no-caiso-gas-commitment-floor; note the flag DEFAULTS ON for CAISO, so omitting it does NOT disable the floor). bidir-intertie + interchange shaping + negative renewable offers + gas-hub-basis overlay, 3-yr. RESULT: basis-only is nearly byte-identical to caiso-21 (basis+floor) on every 2024 axis \u2014 neg-price hours 194 (caiso-21 194; keeper 815; actual ~800), net interchange -19.09 TWh (caiso-21 -17.68; keeper -30.4; actual -32.38), p10 LMP $33.63 (caiso-21 $33.63; keeper $1.40), mean $48.79 (caiso-21 48.8; keeper ~42; actual 35.8), import hours 81.8% vs actual 89.0%; diurnal corr -0.04 (2024), -0.12 (2025), +0.94 (2023 \u2014 static-ladder placeholder, no measured hub so the basis is INACTIVE that year, not informative). 2025 net interchange -25.70 TWh (keeper -38.6; actual -36.16). CONCLUSION: removing the floor barely moved anything, so the DELIVERY BASIS \u2014 not the gas floor \u2014 is the import suppressor on the single-flow bidir node. The multiplicative line-loss markup is too aggressive on the already-capped (8.3 GW) bidir leg: it over-prices imports, collapses the negative tail and net-import magnitude, and drags the diurnal correlation the standalone bidir keeper (caiso-20) flipped POSITIVE back to negative. Next: try wheel-only (additive) delivery or a smaller loss factor on the bidir import legs, grounded as a physical input (rule #12), NOT tuned to the residual; otherwise the diurnal-shape fix (bidir) and the merit-order-restoring basis are mutually exclusive on the capped single-flow node and the keeper stays. See results/calibration/NEXT-caiso-roadmap-2026-06-23.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-23-bidir-basis.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-24-bidir-wheel", "label": "caiso 24 bidir+wheel-only (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-24-bidir-wheel", "definition": "PROBE (failed keeper gate). Bidir WECC intertie + RA gas floor 0.80 + ADDITIVE wheel-only delivery basis on the import legs (the per-tranche OATT PTP wheeling charge of CAISO_IMPORT_DELIVERY_BASIS, WITHOUT its multiplicative loss\u00b7max(hub,0) markup). Decompose follow-up to caiso-22 floor-only / caiso-23 basis-only: tests whether the minimal additive wheel restores the rising delivered-import merit order without over-suppressing imports. It does not \u2014 wheel-only lands essentially on basis-only (2024 net -18.66 vs -19.09, neg 194=194, mean 48.34 vs 48.79) and worse than floor-only on both level (net -22.36, neg 221, mean 46.81) and shape (2024 corr +0.14 < floor-only +0.22; 2025 corr -0.07, not positive every year). Confirms the bidir diurnal-shape fix and any merit-order-restoring delivery basis are mutually exclusive by design on the capped 8.3 GW single-flow node; keeper stays until measured WECC hub diurnal prices land (roadmap option B).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-24-bidir-wheel.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-25-corridor-deliverability", "label": "caiso 25 corridor-deliverability", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-25-corridor-deliverability", "definition": "NEW KEEPER. Adds the measured WECC corridor deliverability limit (--caiso-corridor-flow-limit) on top of the per-hub signed legs: each import corridor's import-direction link flow is capped at the per-(month x hour-of-day) p95 measured net import (an ATC proxy from EIA-930 BA-to-BA interchange; one-sided, export keeps the physical TTC). Fixes the diurnal phase residual -- net-import corr flips POSITIVE both scored years (2024 -0.15->+0.08, 2025 -0.36->+0.03), the first per-hub run with the right diurnal sign -- and fixes 2025 over-import (net -41.1->-36.3 TWh vs actual -36.2). Removing the phantom midday thermal import lifts the body to its honest level (mean 2024 +2.6/2025 +4.9) and drops neg-hours (459->346/193->61): the exposed in-state midday over-pricing residual (next phase), NOT a reason to revert (rule #1). 2024 net now under-imports (-28.3) -- the exposed evening import shortfall. P0->P1, commitment off; 2023 = static-ladder hub fallback but corridor cap active (corr +0.97).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-25-corridor-deliverability.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-intertie", "label": "caiso per hub intertie", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-per-hub-intertie", "definition": "CAISO keeper (structurally-faithful successor to caiso-import-cap-floor): byte-identical config, the ONLY change is the measured WECC intertie parquet now carries the FULL delivered nodal LMP (energy+congestion+loss = MCE+MCC+MCL, GHG excluded) per scheduling point instead of the byte-identical system MCE \u2014 so MALIN (PNW/Mid-C) and PALOVRDE (desert-SW) finally differ (2024 MALIN mean 40.1/313 neg-hrs vs PALOVRDE 33.3/949 neg-hrs). The import injector drops the modeled multiplicative line-loss markup (the measured MCL now carries the real loss) and keeps only the OATT wheel (rules #11/#12). Effect: 2024 mean LMP 42->39.24 toward actual 35.8 (the DIAGNOSIS-caiso-body-overprice midday-floor target improves); 2024 neg-hours 815->873 (actual ~800); 2024 net interchange -30.4->-35.95 TWh (actual -32.4) and 2025 -38.6->-44.18 (actual -36.2) \u2014 over-import GREW because the now-cheaper desert-SW PALOVRDE hub pulls more midday import volume; diurnal phase corr still negative (2024 -0.34). 2023 falls back to the static ladder (OASIS Jan-Feb aged out -> incomplete 8760, unchanged from keeper). Residual / next root cause: the over-import is a discovered downstream issue (import cap / DSW gas coupling), NOT a reason to revert the faithful measured input.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-intertie.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-legs", "label": "caiso per-hub legs + solar-shape (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-per-hub-legs", "definition": "PROBE \u2014 per-hub signed legs (--caiso-per-hub-intertie) WITH --caiso-import-solar-shape still ON, to test whether the solar-shape proxy still adds anything once the measured per-hub Palo Verde hub is doing the work. Verdict: REDUNDANT. Nearly byte-identical to the per-hub-signed keeper on every axis (2024 mean 43.49 vs 43.82, neg 535 vs 459, net \u221232.65 vs \u221232.39, corr \u22120.17 vs \u22120.15; 2025 mean 44.55 vs 44.86, net \u221241.48 vs \u221241.11, corr \u22120.38 vs \u22120.36). solar-shape was a no-OASIS proxy that collapsed the desert-SW import offer toward \u2212$20 to manufacture the negative midday tail; the measured per-hub hub now prints that tail directly, and on a signed node the \u2212$20 import offer mildly re-inflates the very midday over-import the per-hub legs fix (slightly worse corr, +76 neg-hrs). Confirms dropping solar-shape from the keeper (rule #11: prefer the measured input over the proxy). Not a keeper.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-legs.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-signed", "label": "caiso per-hub signed legs", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "caiso-per-hub-signed", "definition": "CAISO KEEPER candidate \u2014 the structural fix for the over-import (priority 1) the per-hub-intertie keeper surfaced. Splits the single pooled WECC_import node into the two REAL signed corridors \u2014 COI/Path-66 at the Malin hub (WECC_PNW \u2192 NP15) and Path-46/WOR at the Palo Verde hub (WECC_DSW \u2192 SP15) \u2014 each a single net direction per hour priced at its OWN measured intertie hub (--caiso-per-hub-intertie). This unifies the bidir node's per-hub netting (each corridor carries one signed flow, so the cheap Palo Verde midday block reverses to EXPORT instead of flooding the 8.3 GW budget) with the hub-price node's per-hub basis (Malin != Palo Verde), which were thought mutually exclusive. The 8.3 GW simultaneous-import cap is preserved as the WECC_import_simultaneous interface limit re-homed onto the two corridor links. solar-shape DROPPED (the measured per-hub Palo Verde hub already prints the negative midday tail it proxied; redundant on a signed node \u2014 confirmed by the per-hub-legs+solar-shape probe). RESULT vs keeper (2024/2025): net interchange \u221236.04/\u221244.17 \u2192 \u221232.39/\u221241.11 TWh (actual \u221232.38/\u221236.16) \u2014 2024 DEAD-ON, over-import SOLVED; diurnal interchange corr \u22120.35/\u22120.60 \u2192 \u22120.15/\u22120.36 (improved, still negative \u2014 the model still imports midday because the cheap hub invites it; a midday-deliverability mechanism is the remaining priority-2 lever). Mean LMP 39.24/43.03 \u2192 43.82/44.86 and neg-hrs 873/407 \u2192 459/193 REGRESSED \u2014 rule #1: this reveals the keeper's better body was partly an OVER-IMPORT ARTIFACT (cheap midday imports suppressed prices / manufactured negatives); the honest body is higher and the residual is now in-state midday over-pricing (DIAGNOSIS-caiso-body-overprice), NOT import, to fix at root cause. 2023 falls back to the static ladder (OASIS gap). Diagnosis: DIAGNOSIS-caiso-perhub-overimport-diurnal-2026-06-23.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-caiso-per-hub-signed.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso2-importnode", "label": "miso2 importnode", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso2-importnode", "definition": "(PROBE) Adds the forecast-grade reference-price interchange node for MISO (Module M4): PJM/SPP/SERC-South seams, each priced from the neighbor's own gas x heat-rate x EIA-930 load shape (HR 12.3/10.0/12.0; cited interface limits 7.3/4.0/3.0 GW), the LP clearing volume on the spread vs MISO's own LMP. Default-on for MISO via REFERENCE_PRICE_DEFAULT_ISOS; PJM opt-in, ERCOT byte-identical. Also corrected HENRY_HUB_TRAJECTORIES[2025] 2.88->3.52 (actual). Determination NOT-YET: the node is structurally correct and measured-anchored, but MISO's modeled LMP is too low (-6/-14/-13%, pre-existing offer-curve level miss), so fed correctly-priced neighbors the seam EXPORTS MISO's over-cheap energy rather than importing -- net interchange does not reach EIA-930 (model NI +0.1/-7.5/+45.0 vs actual -37.9/-23.1/-19.0 TWh; 2024 imports modestly, 2025 over-exports). Root cause for next pass: MISO price level + the absent firm-hydro (Manitoba) import that a gas-margin seam cannot represent. Mechanism stays in (CLAUDE.md #1); seam not tuned to the net-MWh target (#12).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso2-importnode.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso3-reservecoopt", "label": "miso3 reservecoopt", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso3-reservecoopt", "definition": "(PROBE) Adds the in-LP market-wide energy+operating-reserve co-optimization for MISO (scarcity.miso_reserve_coopt_inputs, the MISO analogue of the ERCOT/PJM/NYISO co-opt): requirement = fleet-derived MSSC + 400 MW regulation (~3 GW), VOLL-anchored Reliability-Based Demand Curve to $3,500/MWh, linearised to zero reserve; single footprint-wide reserve family; import node default-on. Determination NOT-YET: the mechanism is structurally correct, live, and sourced (nothing fitted), but FULLY INERT -- reserve_price is exactly $0 in all 26,280 hours, so the energy solution is identical to miso2 (C3a still -9/-17/-14%, net interchange +0.1/-7.5/+45.0 unchanged). The ~3 GW requirement never binds because the perfect-foresight LP holds tens of GW of idle thermal headroom -- the same result the PJM reserve campaign documented. Per CLAUDE.md #1/#11 the requirement was NOT inflated to manufacture scarcity; the structure stays in as the correct baseline. The level miss is carried by market-specific MISO coal offer curves + a firm-hydro (Manitoba) import block (handed off).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso3-reservecoopt.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso4-coal-takeorpay", "label": "miso4 coal takeorpay", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso4-coal-takeorpay", "definition": "(NOT-YET; structurally-correct input, fit-neutral) Replaces MISO's uniform ASSUMED coal take-or-pay with the MEASURED EIA-923 Schedule-5 contracted share per plant (coal_takeorpay_MISO.csv) via coal_takeorpay_from_data, wired into split_coal_tranches (MISO is split-fleet, NOT in CAMPD_BINNING_ISOS, so the task's offer_curve_by_group lever is architecturally inert for MISO \u2014 its coal supply curve is t1/t2/t3 from coal_tranche_*_fuel_passthrough). The sunk first tranche now passes 1-contract_share instead of 100%-sunk, mirroring the binned path's campd_tranche_fuel_frac must-run rule. REFUTES the task premise: measured data shows MISO coal is ~97% contract (tonnage-wtd) \u2014 MORE take-or-pay than ERCOT (~71%), not market-bought \u2014 so the deep sunk tranche is correct and coal offers are NOT the C3a/seam lever. Result is near-baseline (coal 180.9/165.5/231.1 vs miso3 181.2/166.1/231.4; meanLMP 29.25/26.15/37.54 vs 29.15/26.12/37.54 still ~9/15/12% low; net interchange +1.4/-6.6/+46.2 vs miso3 +0.1/-7.5/+45.0, marginally MORE export \u2014 vs actual -37.9/-23.1/-19.0). Only the 2 spot-heavy plants (1167 S:100%, 6213 S:47%) bid their first tranche fuller and back down; the 97% contract majority is unchanged. Per CLAUDE.md #1/#12 the measured input stays in even though it doesn't move the residual; the level/import-seam belongs to the firm-hydro (Manitoba) import block (#810) + seam, not the coal curve. ERCOT and the CAMPD-binned ISOs untouched (flag off, byte-identical). energy-reserve-coopt on (inert), import node default-on.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso4-coal-takeorpay.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso5-firmhydro", "label": "miso5 firmhydro", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso5-firmhydro", "definition": "(KEEPER structure / NOT-YET metrics) Adds a SEPARATE firm-hydro IMPORT block for Manitoba Hydro (~12.3 TWh/yr = 1,400 MW must-flow firm contracted hydro into MISO-North) \u2014 MISO's single largest real import and the structural reason it is a net importer. Firm hydro has no gas x heat-rate analogue, so it is OUTSIDE the gas-margin reference-price seam (PJM/SPP/SERC): a low near-constant offer ($8/MWh, MISO_MANITOBA_FIRM_IMPORT_*) landed directly in MISO-North and floored as must-flow (inject_miso_firm_imports), modeled on the NYISO firm-imports mechanism. Volume from the Manitoba export-contract band, NOT fitted to the residual (#12). Default-ON for MISO (MISO_FIRM_IMPORT_DEFAULT_ISOS); requires --priced-interchange; ERCOT byte-identical. RESULT: net interchange moves toward EIA-930 actual in all 3 yrs (base +0.1/-7.5/+45.0 -> -4.83/-13.31/+39.13 TWh; actual -37.9/-23.1/-19.0) and mean LMP edges DOWN (28.71/25.49/36.78 -> 28.54/25.27/36.40; actual 31.79/30.80/42.85) \u2014 both exactly as predicted (cheap firm hydro raises imports while displacing marginal gas). Gap only HALF closes: the +12.3 TWh import is partly re-exported by the seam because MISO's LMP sits ~6/14/13% low (the pre-existing miso2 root cause), so a correctly-priced seam stays export-biased. Per #1 the correct structure stays in; closing C2/C3a needs the MISO price-LEVEL fix, not a deeper Manitoba block.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso5-firmhydro.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso6-coalclass", "label": "miso6 coalclass", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso6-coalclass", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle miso6_coalclass.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso6-coalclass.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-miso7-zonalload", "label": "miso7 zonalload", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "miso7-zonalload", "definition": "(PROBE) miso3 reserve-coopt config re-solved on current main with all merged MISO structural work \u2014 per-zone hourly load shapes from EIA-930 sub-BA demand (zones = whole sub-BA/LRZ unions, shares 0.285/0.444/0.271, WI->Central/MO->North), RDT asymmetric links (3000 N->S / 2500 S->N), per-zone wind CF shapes, #812 coal take-or-pay, firm-hydro import. Load-weighted LMP 28.98/25.89/37.11 vs actual 31.79/30.80/42.85 (-8.8/-15.9/-13.4%) \u2014 a few tenths closer than static-share miso3 but the gap persists. Scarcity tail still absent (0h >$200 vs 30/37/88) and zones don't separate (N=C, N<->C link still the 12 GW placeholder). Next levers: a binding N<->C corridor limit and a live scarcity tail, not load-share tuning.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-miso7-zonalload.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-neiso-26-ps-diag", "label": "neiso 26 ps diag", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "neiso-26-ps-diag", "definition": "neiso 26 ps-diag (DIAGNOSIS, not a calibration keeper). Byte-faithful re-solve of the neiso-25 fullstop keeper config (--commitment --ct-deployment --hydro-backfill-year 2024 --hydro-eia930-monthly; identical offer curves/overlays/outages, scarcity off, PS adder 0) to surface and root-cause the newly-wired C5b storage-throughput metric. FINDING: NEISO PS under-cycles (2025 model 0.59 vs EIA-930 2.08 TWh, -72%) NOT because of a storage defect but because the energy-only LP collapses the scarcity tail (model >$200 hrs 0/0/14 vs actual 72/43/290; 2025 max $258.98 vs ~$1110). PS round-trip breakeven needs an intraday price ratio >1.25 (RTE 0.80); the model's median daily ratio is 1.15, so PS clears only ~50 days/yr (~20 cycles vs the ~107 needed for 2 TWh) -- energy cap (18.6 GWh) and the $0 adder are non-binding. Step-1 measurement audit: 2023 EIA-930 PS is fully blank and 2024 begins only in Nov (14.8% coverage), so the old +47% '2024 over-cycle' was a 2-month partial scored as a full year; fixed by a coverage gate in eia_loader (storage series <50% reported -> SKIPPED). 2025 under-cycle is a real MODEL MISS handed to the scarcity/winter-gas workstream (daily AGT spot U4 / NEISO reserve scarcity pricing) -- NO storage knob added (claude.md #1/#11/#12). DETERMINATION NOT-YET (C5b storage 2025 + pre-existing C1 CC_REGULAR 2025 +2.15 TWh inherited from neiso-25). See docs/multi-iso/neiso-ps-undercycling-diagnosis-2026-06.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-neiso-26-ps-diag.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-neiso-27-agt-daily", "label": "neiso 27 agt daily", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "neiso-27-agt-daily", "definition": "real daily AGT (EIA-narrative prints + Transco shape), retires demand-convexity proxy", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-neiso-27-agt-daily.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-nyiso-20-daily-coopt", "label": "nyiso 20 daily-coopt-floors", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "nyiso-20-daily-coopt", "definition": "KEEPER. The daily-Transco-gas keeper lineage (nyiso-16/17) combined for the first time with the three validated fix-#3 structural mechanisms: in-LP LOCATIONAL reserve co-optimization (--energy-reserve-coopt, 7 nested NYCA/East/SENY/NYC reserve families), the Long Island in-city self-supply floor (--nyiso-local-selfsupply, NYISO LMIC/local-reliability rule), and firm HQ/Ontario must-flow imports (--nyiso-firm-imports). Full config: --commitment --gas-monthly-actuals --gas-hub-basis-overlay --gas-hub-basis-daily --priced-interchange --energy-reserve-coopt --nyiso-local-selfsupply --nyiso-firm-imports, all 2023+2024+2025. ROOT-CAUSE of the gating winter/January downstate over-pricing: it is the gas-level artifact (the EIA-923 January receipt cost is winter-storm-inflated), NOT an import-topology bug. The measured daily Transco Z6 NY spot de-smears the within-month cold spike, collapsing the January downstate over-price from ~$98 -> $49 (load-weighted Jan residual +44 -> +5.8) and the over-generating NYC fleet from 40 -> 33.6 TWh (toward EIA-923 29). Canonical gate (verdict price_mean): 2023 +19.9% (keeper nyiso-17 +24.0%), 2024 +2.1% (keeper +7.2%, PASS), 2025 +1.2% (keeper +5.4%, PASS) -- BEATS the keeper on price every year; price_shape 2023 0.212 (keeper 0.251), 2024 0.250 (keeper 0.262). 2023 co2 now PASSES (keeper FAIL +7.7%); 2023 sysvol-gas +3.0% (keeper +5.1%); the keeper's ST_GAS +5.05 TWh fuelmix overshoot is gone. Volume bands held within ~1pp of the documented EIA-930/923 import basis floor (2024/25 gas -11/-9%). REMAINING (bounded, documented): the ~+5-7 $/MWh roughly-uniform downstate body over-price is the import/congestion residual bounded by the gas band (PJM $34 > downstate $29-38, so the deep import tranches cannot clear without breaking the EIA-923 gas band) plus the data-blocked flat-annual Iroquois-Transco summer over-level (fix #1(b), needs a paywalled monthly Iroquois Z2 series -- a winter-concentrated/multiplicative reconstruction is mean-preserving on annual gas and only trades summer for winter, so it is not a clean fix). Dec-2024 -27 and the short 2025 summer tail are the coopt-winter-eligibility follow-on (handoff #2). No fitted adders/haircuts; every input measured and forward-reproducible.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-nyiso-20-daily-coopt.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-nyiso-21-family-elig", "label": "nyiso 21 family elig", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "nyiso-21-family-elig", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_21_family_elig.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-nyiso-21-family-elig.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-nyiso-22-gas-offer", "label": "nyiso 22 gas offer", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "nyiso-22-gas-offer", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_22_gas_offer.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-nyiso-22-gas-offer.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-pjm-43-coalbit-marginal", "label": "pjm 43 coalbit marginal", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "pjm-43-coalbit-marginal", "definition": "pjm_43_coalbit_marginal: pjm_42_campdfix keeper + bituminous made marginal/price-responsive \u2014 coal_bit_passthrough_floor 0.76->1.0 (full delivered-cost bid on above-must-run tranches) AND coal_bit_dispatchable (bit per-plant CAMPD must-run floor zeroed; all capacity dispatchable, Pmin=0). PRB/lignite/waste keep take-or-pay floors. Spot-coal contract physics, not tuned to the coal/LMP residual, 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-pjm-43-coalbit-marginal.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-pjm-44-coalbit-sigmoid", "label": "pjm 44 coalbit sigmoid", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "pjm-44-coalbit-sigmoid", "definition": "pjm_44_coalbit_sigmoid_dispatchable: pjm_42_campdfix keeper + coal_bit_dispatchable (bit per-plant must-run floor zeroed, Pmin=0) but the gas-keyed bituminous sigmoid RETAINED (floor 0.76 -> ceil 1.32, mid 3.40), NOT flattened to full cost. Isolates dispatchability with the gas-tied offer intact: cheap gas discounts bit to hold merit, dear gas marks it up to back down, 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-pjm-44-coalbit-sigmoid.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-pjm-46-online-pmin", "label": "pjm 46 online pmin", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "pjm-46-online-pmin", "definition": "Coal-operations rebuild steps 1-2 on the PJM keeper: coal_takeorpay_from_data (step 1, measured EIA-923 Sch-5 sunk-fuel share) + coal_mustrun_online_pmin (step 2, coal must-run band sized to the measured online-net-MW synchronization Pmin / mustrun_online_pct ~27% cap-wtd, vs the all-hours available-CF mustrun_pct ~44%). Shrinking the cheap sub-cost band moves coal into full-cost tranches so it price-follows: vs the keeper, mean-LMP C3a now PASSes all 3 years (2025 -14.5%->-6.0%, fixing the suppression) and 2023 coal +9.2%->+0.1%; it overshoots to 2024 coal -7.9% / 2025 gas +11.4%, the next root cause (step-3 SRMC tranche + reserve co-opt). Still NOT-YET. Solved 2023-25 (serial per-year solves, merged).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-pjm-46-online-pmin.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-pjm-47-ps-adder", "label": "pjm 47 ps adder", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "pjm-47-ps-adder", "definition": "pjm_47_psdiag: pjm_46 online-pmin keeper, PS DISPATCH ADDER RETIRED (PUMPED_STORAGE_DISPATCH_ADDER_BY_ISO PJM $10 -> {}; resolves to 0.0). The $10 had been fitted to a mis-measured EIA-923 PS *net* figure (round-trip loss ~2.6 TWh) read as gross discharge; true discharge throughput is ~7-10 TWh (net*RTE/(1-RTE); PJM Hydro - conventional HY). PS now arbitrages on physical RTE 0.80. All other flags = keeper. 2023 only [merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves]", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-pjm-47-ps-adder.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-pjm-48-sync-srmc", "label": "pjm 48 sync srmc", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "pjm-48-sync-srmc", "definition": "pjm 47 sync-srmc: PJM coal rebuild step 3a (online%-scaled synchronization forcing) on the FULL re-derived thermal tranches. Steps 1+2+3a stacked on the keeper: coal_takeorpay_from_data (measured EIA-923 Sch-5 sunk share) + coal_mustrun_online_pmin (min-load band sized to the measured online-net-MW Pmin) + coal_sync_srmc_tranche (split that band by the contract share into a fuel-free _mustrun floor + a full-SRMC _sync band, both forced ON via min_gen). NEW this run: the forcing is ONLINE%-SCALED via a re-derived plant-level online_frac column (hours any unit synchronized / 8760) \u2014 supercriticals (~1.0) held all 8760 h, two-shifting cyclers forced only in their top-online_frac hours by system load (mirrors the CT must-run load-shaping). The thermal_tranches_PJM.csv was fully re-derived 2023-25, which also corrected the pre-NaN-fix online_hours / committed_pct / mustrun_online_pct for every multi-unit coal+gas plant (CLAUDE.md #12 accurate data). Subbit sigmoid retuned to v3 (ceil 1.65->2.10 / slope 1.4->3.0). RESULTS vs pjm_46 keeper (-7.9/+0.1/+7.5): online%-scaling FIXES the 2023 over-hold (coal +5.5%->-0.1%, all classes in C2 tol); the accurate re-derive (lower committed band) deepens the cheapest-gas 2024 under-run to -9.3% (bit floor VERIFIED saturated: 0.68->0.76->0.90 leaves 2024 bit at -9.4%, so it is NOT an offer-curve lever \u2014 the residual is the step-3b reserve co-opt gap that carries the dear-gas/scarcity volume, memory-blocked per Thread D); 2025 +8.7% (subbit cyclers 876/879 +25% the dominant per-class miss, dear-gas reserve co-opt the lever). Structurally the most complete coal representation (steps 1-3a); residual attributed to the blocked step-3b, not offer-curve fudging. Still NOT-YET pending step-3b. Solved 2023-25 serial, merged.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-pjm-48-sync-srmc.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-run151-coal-prb-sigmoid", "label": "run151 coal prb sigmoid", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "run151-coal-prb-sigmoid", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-run151-coal-prb-sigmoid.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-run152-waha-haircut", "label": "run152 waha haircut", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "run152-waha-haircut", "definition": "run151 coal-keeper recipe + ERCOT zonal gas with the MEASURED contract haircut (each zone's Waha hub basis scaled by its EIA-923 Schedule-5 gas spot share; West 78% spot). This re-grounds the West/Permian delivered-gas depth on measured data instead of the -0.50 scalar floor, which was DROPPED as non-physical: the contract data shows the CT over-runners buy 100% spot Waha gas, i.e. they genuinely see ~$0 gas, so a floor overstates their fuel (CLAUDE.md #11). The haircut is the honest gas-side correction but small (trims CT_PEAKER from +82.8% to +68.6% in 2024). The remaining CT_PEAKER over-run (C1: 2024 +5.63 TWh, 2025 +4.45 TWh) is an open MODEL MISS for direction 3 (peaker cost/classification): Morgan Creek 3492 is a DFO distillate peaker mispriced on gas, and the GT peakers bid baseload on cheap gas because HR*fuel+VOM understates their low-CF startup/non-fuel cost. LMP MAE 27.9/15.2/11.3 (no regression). See docs/ercot-ct-waha-offer-floor-2026-06.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-run152-waha-haircut.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-run153-waha-haircut-perplant", "label": "run153 waha haircut perplant", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "run153-waha-haircut-perplant", "definition": "run152 with the Waha contract haircut made PER-PLANT instead of zone-average: each gas unit's hub basis is scaled by ITS OWN EIA-923 spot share, so 100%-spot units (Permian Basin, Laredo) keep the full discount while a 100%-contract unit in the same zone (Ector County, share 0.0) loses it entirely. More physically faithful than the zone mean AND a better fit every year (CLAUDE.md #11/#12): CT_PEAKER 2023 -17.6% (was -25.9%), 2024 +48.7% (was +68.6%), 2025 +62.7% (was +66.8%). 358/1124 gas units haircut (mean share 0.51). LMP MAE 27.8/15.2/11.3 (no regression); C2 gas/coal family, C4 dispatch, C5a CO2 pass. The remaining CT_PEAKER over-run (2024 +4.0 TWh) is the open direction-3 MODEL MISS (the 100%-spot Permian peakers genuinely see cheap Waha gas, so the fix is peaker economics/classification, not gas price). The -0.50 floor is NOT used (dropped as non-physical). See docs/ercot-ct-waha-offer-floor-2026-06.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-run153-waha-haircut-perplant.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-23-run154-netload-gas-laredo", "label": "run154 netload gas laredo", "date": "2026-06-23", "shorthand": "run154-netload-gas-laredo", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-23-run154-netload-gas-laredo.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-caiso-cc-econ-10", "label": "caiso 26 cc-econ-cut (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "caiso-cc-econ-10", "definition": "PROBE (rejected) \u2014 Task A: CC_REGULAR+CC_CHP econ_low/econ_high -0.10 to lower the in-state midday body off the caiso-25 corridor keeper. REJECTED: breaks the won diurnal structure (net-import corr flips negative both scored years, 2024 +0.08->-0.03, 2025 +0.03->-0.08) and under-imports (2024 net -28.34->-24.70, below the -28 floor) \u2014 cheaper CC displaces imports in the midday/shoulder hours the corridor cap fixed. Also non-specific (lowers midday/evening/overnight ~uniformly, pulls the matched 2023 evening under actual) and never recovers the negative tail (neg-hrs unchanged 271/24). Confirms residual #2 is structural (model short midday after phantom imports correctly removed), not an offer-curve level. Keeper unchanged. See DIAGNOSIS-caiso-cc-offer-midday-2026-06-24.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-caiso-cc-econ-10.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-caiso-perhub-shaping-diag", "label": "caiso perhub-shaping diag (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "caiso-perhub-shaping-diag", "definition": "(PROBE) Keeper config + --caiso-per-hub-intertie + --interchange-shaping. Tests the grounded import-volume lever (measured EIA-930 availability envelope). Fixes import HOURS (95.9% vs 85.9% actual) and diurnal shape, but OVERCORRECTS volume: net imports under-shoot every year (21.2/24.7/31.7 vs actual 28.9/32.4/36.2 TWh) and gas over-burns vs EIA-923. Not a keeper; the correct fix is per-hub SIGNED legs (see HANDOFF-caiso-import-volume-and-2023-data-2026-06-24). 2023 uses the static-ladder fallback for hub LMP (OASIS data aged out), shaped only by the EIA-930 envelope.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-caiso-perhub-shaping-diag.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-miso8-campd-binning", "label": "miso8 campd-binning (pre-tz ablation)", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "miso8-campd-binning", "definition": "ABLATION (binning-only, pre-timezone) \u2014 SUPERSEDED as keeper by `miso8 tz-localclock` (PR #853), which adds the MISO UTC-vs-local clock fix on top of this same binning. Keep as the isolation point for the per-plant binning effect alone. CLOCK CAVEAT: this bundle was solved on the old UTC demand clock, so its diurnal/shape comparison against main's now-local-clock benchmark shows a ~5-6h phantom phase error \u2014 that is the clock artifact the tz fix removed, NOT a model result; only the year-mean LMP level (clock-independent) is a valid read here. STRUCTURAL change isolated: MISO moved off the legacy equal-width aggregate_fleet path onto per-plant CAMPD tranche binning (now in CAMPD_BINNING_ISOS) \u2014 one LP unit per plant with must-run/committed/peaking tranches from the new thermal_tranches_MISO.csv (44 coal plants, measured online Pmin ~20-30% nameplate; CC per-plant committed bands), plus the all-ISO biomass measured must-run change (#844). Coal now holds its overnight/morning take-or-pay floor instead of cycling to zero. System LMP level: model 31.49/27.99/39.34 vs actual ~31.79/30.80/42.85 ($/MWh; -0.9%/-9.1%/-8.2% vs legacy miso7's -8.8%/-15.9%/-13.4%) \u2014 C3a mean-LMP PASS. NOT-YET: governance unattested + open MODEL MISSes that are NOT the binning \u2014 absent scarcity tail (0h >$200 vs 30/37/88) and the import/export-node seam (2025 sign-flips to net export, driving 2025 coal +23%); zones still don't separate (N=C, 12 GW N<->C placeholder). PRB passthrough still flat (no MISO COAL_SIGMOID_DEFAULTS entry) \u2014 a separate offer-curve lever; a cheap-gas PRB discount would push the already-low LMP further from actual, so not the next fix.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-miso8-campd-binning.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-miso8-tz-localclock", "label": "miso8 tz localclock", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "miso8-tz-localclock", "definition": "miso8 tz-localclock: fixes a confirmed UTC-vs-local clock misalignment AND picks up per-plant CAMPD tranche binning (MISO added to CAMPD_BINNING_ISOS, PR #843). MISO renewable CF was on local time but both demand sources were UTC (~5h CDT / ~6h CST ahead), pairing midday solar / nocturnal wind against trough demand and cycling coal PRB + CC down across 03:00-13:00 where CAMPD shows them hot. Fix: system demand re-sourced from the EIA-930 MISO hourly frame Demand column (was the UTC demand-profiles parquet; identical 641 TWh / peak, only the clock moves), and the sub-BA zonal shares mapped UTC->local via that same frame, so demand and renewable CF share one local wall clock (offset derived from the clock, not the fit; rule #12). RESULT: summer coal diurnal now bottoms ~local 05:00 and ramps to an 18:00 peak, tracking the CAMPD ramp (bottom 04:00 / peak 19:00) at 0.995 shape correlation vs the prior phantom-surplus midday cliff; C3a mean-LMP and C3b shape PASS. Same miso7/miso3 reference-coopt config (reference-price interface + priced interchange + firm hydro imports, all default-on). NOT-YET overall: C1/C2 still under on gas/coal volume (CT_PEAKER ~-26 TWh prices out) and the scarcity tail (C3c) is absent \u2014 both pre-existing MISO misses, not tz-related.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-miso8-tz-localclock.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-neiso-28-hydro-codramp", "label": "neiso 28 hydro codramp", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "neiso-28-hydro-codramp", "definition": "neiso-27 + EIA-930 hydro repin + storage vintage ramp + renewable/storage retirement ramp", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-28-hydro-codramp.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-neiso-29-biomass-mustrun", "label": "neiso 29 biomass mustrun", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "neiso-29-biomass-mustrun", "definition": "neiso-28 + biomass measured must-run injection + like-for-like total comparison", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-29-biomass-mustrun.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-neiso-30-other-carry", "label": "neiso 30 other carry", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "neiso-30-other-carry", "definition": "(DIAGNOSTIC; keeper stays neiso-29) neiso-29 + OTHER residual class vintage-carry (2025 OTHER 2.61->2.78 TWh, measured EIA-923 like biomass): small correct fix (C1 +3.64->+3.56, C2 +3.0%->+2.5%). Solved on current main, so it also surfaces a8b0e55 (CC nameplate ~75%->92%) dropping NEISO prices 13-18% into under-prediction (C3a -15.9/-14.0/-8.1%) and storage -76% -- a SEPARATE NEISO CC-availability item, not the biomass/OTHER work.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-neiso-30-other-carry.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-23-li-oil", "label": "nyiso 23 li oil", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "nyiso-23-li-oil", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_23_li_oil_fix.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-23-li-oil.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-24-import-recon", "label": "nyiso 24 import recon", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "nyiso-24-import-recon", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_24_import_recon.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-24-import-recon.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-25-scarcity-merit", "label": "nyiso 25 rcpf-steep (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "nyiso-25-scarcity-merit", "definition": "PROBE (rejected, not a keeper). Tests the bucket-A #1 scarcity hypothesis: steepen the NYISO locational RCPF reserve demand curves from the linear-ramp-to-zero stand-in to the published top-25%-span shape (critical_mw = 0.75 x requirement; FERC ER21-502 sets the NYCA 30-min $750 max at 1,965 = 0.75 x 2,620), plus the Ravenswood steam-HR correction. Finding: the steeper published curve DEEPENS the downstate tail faithfully (NYC max $138->$890, 2023 14h >$300) but does NOT add C3c tail HOURS (2024 0h, 2025 12h, unchanged) because the tail COUNT is incidence-gated by the model\u2019s too-loose downstate reserve headroom (idle NYC peakers provide ample reserve; perfect-foresight import over-service backs down downstate gas), not by the demand-curve slope. The deeper few-hour spikes push C3a mean LMP to +12% (over the +-8% band) for no C3c gain. CONCLUSION: the RCPF demand-curve SHAPE is not the C3c lever; the lever is downstate reserve INCIDENCE (locational headroom / import discipline) \u2014 the documented next structural step, out of scope as it would touch the nyiso-24 import reconciliation. The curve steepening is therefore NOT carried into the keeper (nyiso 25 steam-merit), which retains only the Ravenswood merit-order fix.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-25-scarcity-merit.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-nyiso-25-steam-merit", "label": "nyiso 25 steam merit", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "nyiso-25-steam-merit", "definition": "KEEPER. Re-solves the nyiso-24 import-reconciliation keeper config (byte-identical flags) with ONE model-side data correction (data.fleet.MIXED_FACILITY_STEAM_HR): the Ravenswood (plant 2500, NYC) mixed CC+ST facility had its ~1.7 GW steam units inheriting the 8.8 MMBtu/MWh plant-blended heat rate (the efficient combined cycle\u2019s efficiency leaking into the steam row), so the big NYC steam unit cleared AHEAD of idle NYC combined cycle on merit (the 2023 CC_REGULAR -4.06 / ST_GAS +3.55 TWh inversion). Recovered the steam units\u2019 own HR (9.5) by backing the CC (~7.5) out of the 8.8 generation-weighted blend; restores the physically-correct merit order (CC ahead of steam). CC_REGULAR improves EVERY year (2023 -4.06->-3.47, 2024 -3.21->-2.61, 2025 -1.20->-0.6) and the 2023 ST_GAS over-run shrinks (+3.55->+2.76). A rule-#11 measured-data correction, forward-reproducible, not fitted to a residual. Tradeoff (kept per rule #1): C3a 2025 mean LMP +9.3% (just over +-8%) because import-constrained NYC over-relies on Ravenswood steam as marginal \u2014 a discovered symptom of the NYC import-incidence root cause (out of scope), not a reason to revert the correct HR. The RCPF scarcity tail (C3c) is unchanged: it is incidence-gated, not curve-gated (see the nyiso 25 rcpf-steep PROBE). Determination NOT-YET (the hard EIA-930/923 gas basis floor, ledgered).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-nyiso-25-steam-merit.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-24-pjm-49-seam-hr", "label": "pjm 49 seam hr", "date": "2026-06-24", "shorthand": "pjm-49-seam-hr", "definition": "(DIAGNOSTIC) pjm 48 coal keeper + two grounded reference-price seam corrections. (1) hr_by_year: MISO/NYISO priced per backcast year from their OWN measured realized LMP (MISO 12.38/13.92/12.03, NYISO 9.66/12.92/14.67), replacing the single 3-year-mean marginal_heat_rate that over-priced the dear-gas year. (2) Carolinas HR 13.5->11.6, reconciled to its OWN documented ~$30/MWh SERC-bilateral basis ($30/$2.54/1.02; 13.5 gave $34, +14% over, and was the highest HR of any neighbor for a cheaper region PJM net-imports from). Both rule #12 (measured/reconciled-documented > compensating estimate); forecast byte-identical. RESULT: net interchange 2023 +22.5 / 2024 +31.2 / 2025 +48.5 vs target +40/+32.7/+18 (MAE 16.5 vs 15.7 baseline); 2024 now near-perfect, Carolinas direction corrected (2024 -4.8 vs measured -6.4), gas improves 2023 (+1.9 vs +5.6) and 2025 (+10.4 vs +12.1). The MAE is marginally worse only because the fixes remove the compensating Carolinas over-export + NYISO-2023 over-price that were MASKING the real bug, now cleanly isolated: the model UNDER-clears export to MISO/NYISO (2023 MISO +17.6 vs measured +35.3, NYISO +4.3 vs +18.5) -- the $2 hurdle exceeds the ~$1.5 mean PJM-MISO spread, so the energy-spread seam can't reproduce PJM's large firm exports. Next: (1) MISO/NYISO under-export (hurdle/convex-slide), (2) add the TVA/LGEE import seam (~-8 TWh/yr), (3) anchor NYISO to the PJM-NY border not the NYC-inflated system avg, (4) 2025 EIA-930 sits ~15 TWh below PJM's per-tie sum -- a measured-source basis gap. pjm 48 stays the PJM keeper. Merged 2023-25 from parallel per-year solves. See bundle SUMMARY-seam-structure.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-24-pjm-49-seam-hr.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-159-multiproduct-as-coopt", "label": "159 multiproduct as coopt", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "159-multiproduct-as-coopt", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 159.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-159-multiproduct-as-coopt.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-caiso-26-gas-commodity", "label": "caiso 26 gas-commodity-verify", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "caiso-26-gas-commodity", "definition": "VERIFICATION RUN (not a new keeper). 3-year HEAD reproduction of the keeper config (--gas-hub-basis-overlay + --caiso-import-gas-coupling + --caiso-per-hub-intertie + --caiso-corridor-flow-limit) confirming both requested levers are ALREADY implemented and active: (PRIMARY) commodity-spot gas / lever B reprices every gas unit at Henry-Hub-month + measured SoCal/PG&E citygate basis (data IN REPO, gas_basis_by_iso_month.csv; gas 2024 F923 $4.31 -> citygate $3.38; 1139 units, 12/12 months) and drops the F923 pipeline reservation as sunk; (SECONDARY) per-hub signed legs (two corridors Malin/COI->NP15, PaloVerde/Path-46->SP15, per-hub basis + netting + corridor deliverability cap). Determination: the body stays +25-41% over actuals (2024 system p50 $45.77 vs RT median $33.99) because even at the commodity spot the gas-CC SRMC floor (HR~7.3 x $3.38 + CARB ~$13 ~= $42) STRUCTURALLY EXCEEDS the actual median -- re-pricing gas cannot reach $34 (confirms 2026-06-19 lever-B diagnosis pt #4). Gas vol: 2023 77.9~=76.0 ok; 2024 77.6 vs 67.7 over, but that is a +7 TWh over-gen drift (under-import + solar-over), not a lever-B artifact. Net interchange -29.7/-28.9/-39.7 vs -28.9/-32.4/-36.2. Marginally lower body than the stale-sha keeper (2024 NP15 47.4->46.0), no regression -> keeper 2026-06-23-caiso-25-corridor-deliverability stands. See results/calibration/DETERMINATION-caiso-gas-commodity-and-perhub-2026-06-25.md.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-caiso-26-gas-commodity.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-caiso-28-stgas-hrfix", "label": "caiso 28 stgas hrfix", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "caiso-28-stgas-hrfix", "definition": "CAISO keeper. Fixes the ST_GAS/CT_PEAKER merit inversion: AES Alamitos (315) and Huntington Beach (335) are pure-steam OTC units whose EIA-923 plant heat rate was CC-blended by a colocated CCGT under the same ORIS (315->8.49, 335->7.33, both below the CT_PEAKER median ~10.07), so they cleared ahead of CA peakers. MIXED_FACILITY_STEAM_HR recovers their measured pure-steam value 11.85 (sister plant Ormond Beach 350, same AES Southland OTC fleet, no CC). Result: ST_GAS drops into band (~4.5->~1.1 TWh vs actual 1.38; off the C1 fail list both 2023/2024) and system CO2 tightens (-0.6%/+1.4%/+5.3%, all PASS). Benchmark uses the EIA-930 geo+biomass gas fold-in deflation (classFull gas ~74/73/70 TWh). Reproduces the run-25 corridor-deliverability keeper config (gas-hub-basis-overlay, per-hub intertie + corridor deliverability cap, import gas coupling, RA gas commitment floor 0.80, negative renewable offers) plus the two fixes. CAVEAT: CT_PEAKER stays under-dispatched (~0.4 vs ~4 actual) and CC_REGULAR now slightly over (+3 TWh) \u2014 the freed ST_GAS energy is absorbed by cheaper CC, not peakers; a separate CC-over-dispatch / missing-CT-reliability-commitment gap to pursue, not a reason to revert the grounded HR fix (rule #11).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-caiso-28-stgas-hrfix.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-caiso-29-ctfloor-localra", "label": "caiso 29 ctfloor localra", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "caiso-29-ctfloor-localra", "definition": "CAISO 29: coal-cogen BTM fix (Argus 10684 Industrial-CHP routed through steam-following holdout, ends 0.38 TWh phantom grid coal) + temperature-driven CT_PEAKER local-RA reliability floor (NOAA-TMAX-keyed evening commitment, regressed from measured CAMPD CT CF). CT_CHP EOR over-dispatch + import/total-gas drift diagnosed, deferred.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-caiso-29-ctfloor-localra.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-caiso-29-forward-seam", "label": "caiso 29 forward-seam", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "caiso-29-forward-seam", "definition": "FORWARD WECC-tie seam (G8): swaps the caiso-25 keeper's two MEASURED levers for forecast-native analogues. (1) --caiso-intertie-reference-price prices each per-hub corridor from (HH[year]+gas_basis)\u00d7marginal_HR\u00d7load_shape -- the same reference-price interface PJM/MISO use, desert-SW@Palo-Verde on NET load (midday solar trough), PNW@Malin on gross load -- instead of the measured OASIS hub LMP. (2) --caiso-corridor-atc-forward caps corridor import at TTC\u00d7posted-ATC-frac\u00d7forward CISO-solar derate instead of the measured p95 envelope. Measured hub LMP + p95 kept ONLY as backcast validation (compare_caiso_intertie_formula_vs_measured.py); nothing pinned to the realization (honesty gate #10/#12). Validation: 2024 NAILS net import (model -32.2 vs actual -32.4 TWh) at desert-SW diurnal corr 0.96 with NO measured-LMP input, mean LMP 42.7 (vs measured keeper 44.9, closer to actual 33-36). 2023 over-imports (-39.6 vs -28.9; the data-blocked year). 2025 under-imports/over-prices (-22.0 vs -36.2 TWh, mean 62.2 vs ~34) -- the documented WECC gas-insensitivity (Malin/Palo-Verde are hydro/solar-set, do NOT rise with Henry Hub; the SPP effect), the honest forward residual kept per #1, NOT closed with a measured pin. P0\u2192P1, commitment off. Compare to measured keeper caiso-25-corridor-deliverability.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-caiso-29-forward-seam.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-caiso-29-hsl-curtailment", "label": "caiso 29 hsl curtailment", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "caiso-29-hsl-curtailment", "definition": "caiso stgas-hrfix keeper config + uncurtailed-VRE wiring for the no-HSL years (renewables.py _UNCURTAILED_FALLBACK_ISOS). CAISO 2023/24 already used a built HSL parquet (delivered + reported curtailment); 2025's curtailment workbook is partial (only reaches May), so 2025 now gets the gross-up uncurtailed potential (delivered grossed up by the 2024 HSL reference rate: wind 1.13%, solar 6.63%) instead of net-of-curtailment delivered. FINDING: CAISO models ~0% endogenous curtailment in EVERY year, including the unchanged HSL years 2023/24 (model solar curt 0.0 vs reported 2.5-3.2 TWh) \u2014 the LP absorbs surplus solar via negative renewable offers and exports it through the priced WECC intertie rather than curtailing. This is a pre-existing CAISO characteristic, unchanged by this wiring; making CAISO curtailment endogenous (tightening export/ATC so surplus binds) is a separate investigation. NOT-YET: confounded by the freshly-merged caiso-intertie code in main (transmission/pricing), not this change.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-caiso-29-hsl-curtailment.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-miso10-nuclear-refuel-derate", "label": "miso10 nuclear refuel derate", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "miso10-nuclear-refuel-derate", "definition": "miso10 nuclear-refuel-derate: miso9 (pjm-seam-hranchor) PLUS one new structural lever \u2014 a MEASURED per-year nuclear refueling/maintenance availability overlay for MISO. MISO was the ONLY multi-year ISO with no nuclear availability overlay (CAMPD/CEMS is fossil-only), so nuclear ran flat ~97.9 TWh / ~97% CF EVERY year vs measured 87.2/90.4/90.7 \u2014 a systematic +10.7/+7.5/+7.2 TWh over-injection that filled the bottom of the stack. The lever adds NUCLEAR_MONTHLY_CF_BY_YEAR['MISO'] derived by scripts/derive_nuclear_monthly_cf.py (monthly CF = fleet EIA-923 net gen / fleet pmax x hours, capped at 1.0), the SAME accepted mechanism already in ERCOT/CAISO/PJM/NYISO/NEISO \u2014 an exogenous physical-availability event (the staggered ~18-24mo refueling cadence; rule #12 'unit outage windows'), applied as an availability cap not a generation pin. RESULT: nuclear now lands at measured 87.15/90.20/90.59 TWh (was flat ~97.9) \u2014 nuclear volume error 2024 +7.5 -> -0.2 TWh, with the real refueling seasonality. PASS C3a mean-LMP (31.1/29.2/39.8), C3b shape, C4 dispatch-r, C6 governance. OPEN MISSES (documented, not masked; the lever is kept on rule-#1 faithfulness grounds even though it worsens the import residual): (1) the freed ~7-11 TWh/yr is backfilled by MORE imports in 2023/24 (net import 2024 48.3->52.3, 2023 63.6->69.3 vs actual 23.1/37.9), because the priced seam undercuts the next domestic unit \u2014 this UNMASKS the over-import as the dominant injection error (next lever: SPP/South seam anchor, task Part 4), it does not cause it; (2) 2025 net-interchange sign-flips to net export (+3.4 vs actual -19.0; import-hrs 45.8% vs 79.2%) driven by the persistent 2025 coal over-run (236.8 vs 192-201) \u2014 a coal-offer root cause nuclear doesn't touch; (3) CT_PEAKER stays a real ~16 TWh merit-order under-gen (classifier verified correct, see miso9 CT-note correction); recovers only once over-import is fixed. Solved per-year (2023/2024/2025 separate processes, merged into one bundle) because a 3-year per-plant MISO LP OOMs at 16 GB; MISO backcast has no cross-year dependency, so per-year == combined.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-miso10-nuclear-refuel-derate.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-miso11-spp-seam-anchor", "label": "miso11 spp seam anchor", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "miso11-spp-seam-anchor", "definition": "miso11 spp-seam-anchor: combined keeper on current main folding in every settled MISO lever (PJM per-year HR anchor, EIA-923 nuclear refueling CF, firm-hydro imports, per-plant CAMPD binning) PLUS a new per-year MEASURED SPP neighbor heat-rate anchor (hr_by_year={2023:9.24,2024:10.65,2025:7.7}), derived by derive_neighbor_hr_by_year.py from SPP's realized RT LMP/HH (actual_lmp_hourly_SPP.parquet) \u2014 same method as PJM. The flat 10.0 over-priced wind-heavy SPP in dear-gas 2025 (measured 7.7), flipping MISO to a fake net EXPORT; the cheaper measured SPP RESTORES the correct net-import direction. RESULT (net interchange, EIA +=export): 2025 +3.4 export (miso10) -> -6.7 IMPORT (actual -19.0; import-hrs 45.8->52.9%, RMSE ~3600->2049) = 2025 export-flip FIXED; 2024 -52.3->-49.8 (actual -23.1); 2023 -69.3->-71.9 WORSE (measured 2023 HR 9.24<10.0 imports more \u2014 kept on faithfulness, rule #1/#11). Also renders against the CORRECTED benchmark (generalized EIA-930 geo/biomass fold-in deflation now live: MISO gas actual ~12.8 TWh lower, so the gas residual reflects true natural gas). PASS C3a/C3b/C4/C6; NOT-YET overall. DOMINANT remaining miss now ISOLATED: systematic net OVER-import all years (2023 71.9 vs 37.9) = import-node LEVEL / interface deliverability (real TTC vs placeholder), not per-year seam pricing \u2014 the next lever. Other open: 2025 coal over-run (231.7 vs 192-201; dear-gas coal-offer, after import level); CT_PEAKER merit under-gen (recovers once imports stop undercutting + scarcity tail); scarcity tail absent. South/SOCO kept structural (no market). Solved per-year (OOM) + merged; per-year==combined.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-miso11-spp-seam-anchor.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-miso12-seam-deliverability", "label": "miso12 seam deliverability", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "miso12-seam-deliverability", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle seam_deliverability.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-miso12-seam-deliverability.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-miso9-pjm-seam-hranchor", "label": "miso9 pjm seam hranchor", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "miso9-pjm-seam-hranchor", "definition": "miso9 pjm-seam-hranchor: first MISO keeper on current main (picks up the ISO-generic CC-tranche PRs #846/#857) PLUS a per-year MEASURED PJM neighbor heat-rate anchor (hr_by_year={2023:11.2,2024:13.49,2025:12.18}), derived by derive_neighbor_hr_by_year.py from PJM's realized RT LMP/HH, replacing the flat 3-year mean 12.3. ISOLATED 2024 test: net over-import 60.3->48.3 TWh, interchange RMSE 4592->3259, import-hrs 97.7->92.8% (actual 90.9%). PASS C3a/C3b/C4/C6; NOT-YET overall. NOT calibrated yet \u2014 known structural misses (next levers): (1) over-import still dominant all years (2023 63.6 vs 37.9; SPP/South seams flat/un-anchored + import-node over-serves); (2) 2025 export-flip + coal over-run (235 vs 192, model net-export +7.5 vs actual -19); (3) nuclear over-ran +7-10/yr, INVARIANT at 97.9 TWh ~97% CF on THIS run (capacity is correct 11.5GW/13 units; the historic CAMPD outage source is fossil-only so refueling/maintenance outages weren't derated) \u2014 NOW ADDRESSED by miso10 (EIA-923-derived NUCLEAR_MONTHLY_CF_BY_YEAR['MISO'] availability overlay; nuclear lands at measured 87.2/90.2/90.6 TWh); (4) CT_PEAKER CORRECTION: a REAL merit-order under-gen (model 3 vs EIA-923 actual simple-cycle GT/IC 16.9/18.95/~19, NOT 26.5; capacity exists at 37.8GW gas_ct). The classifier is correct/consistent on both sides (fleet plant_group + EIA-923 benchmark both use plant_taxonomy.classify_plant; CT/CA/CS/CC->CC_REGULAR, only GT/IC->CT_PEAKER) \u2014 NO CC->CT contamination; the MISO-only ct_mustrun_floor sum=18.95=benchmark (the '114 TWh' was the NATIONWIDE sum). --ct-mustrun-per-plant correctly rejected as a rule-#12 actuals-pin, NOT a classification bug; CT recovers once over-import is fixed; (5) COAL_BIT/COAL_PRB/ST_GAS under (displaced by the over-import + nuclear-over). NO unmet demand (slack=0; the 'missing' gen is over-import). Dashboard NOTE: the headline fuel-mix gas row now re-levels its benchmark to the grid-delivered EIA-923 classFull basis (render_calibration_html fix) instead of the EIA-930 NG series, which folded in biomass+other (~6.5 TWh) the model books separately. Solved per-year (OOM workaround) + merged; per-year==combined (no cross-year dependency).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-miso9-pjm-seam-hranchor.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-neiso-31-cc-minload", "label": "neiso 31 cc minload", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "neiso-31-cc-minload", "definition": "NEISO CC_REGULAR committed min-load tranche re-anchored 0.92->1.27 (=econ_high) per measured CAMPD min-stable-load HR (1.30x avg vs full-load 0.93x); min-load priced at-or-above full-load SRMC, removes wall-masked cheap min-load block. a8b0e55 kept ON. vs neiso-30.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-31-cc-minload.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-neiso-32-btm-solar", "label": "neiso 32 btm solar", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "neiso-32-btm-solar", "definition": "vs neiso-31: dispatch-identical (CC min-load 1.27 re-anchor, a8b0e55 ON, biomass must-run, OTHER vintage-carry, measured AGT daily gas overlay all unchanged). Change is a BENCHMARK/attestation reconcile of NEISO solar: model & benchmark both on EIA-930 grid basis (already via actuals_source); documents that EIA-923 ISNE SUN (3.70/4.53 TWh) includes ~2.8-3.2 TWh distribution-connected/net-metered BTM PV netted into the EIA-930 net-load demand series, excluded from both sides by construction. Energy balance unchanged.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-32-btm-solar.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-neiso-33-no-ctfloor", "label": "neiso 33 no ctfloor", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "neiso-33-no-ctfloor", "definition": "vs neiso-32 (keeper): CT AS/RUC out-of-merit deployment-floor overlay REMOVED (ct_deployment_overlay OFF); every other flag/code path byte-identical. Determination-neutral AND dispatch-neutral: the overlay only ever injected 0.076/0.041/0.054 TWh/yr (<0.1% of generation), so removing it leaves gas-family 54.23/58.81/61.60 TWh, system mean LMP 33.95/38.91/66.70 $/MWh and CO2 unchanged, and the verdict stays NOT-YET with the IDENTICAL basis (undocumented fuelmix / price_tail / storage). The overlay was the calibration log's 'most answer-injecting' measured-CEMS floor yet did not flip any gate (CT_PEAKER is structurally under-produced ~0.5-0.9 TWh/yr by the energy-only LP \u2014 a reserves/local-reliability MODEL MISS the 0.04 TWh floor never closed). Removing it is strictly cleaner per CLAUDE.md #12 (one fewer measured-outcome injection) at zero cost to the backcast.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NEISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-neiso-33-no-ctfloor.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-26-cc-nameplate", "label": "nyiso 26 cc nameplate", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-26-cc-nameplate", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_26_cc-nameplate.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-26-cc-nameplate.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-27-cc-offer", "label": "nyiso 27 cc offer", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-27-cc-offer", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle nyiso_27_cc-offer.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-27-cc-offer.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-28-native-hr", "label": "nyiso 28 native-hr (PROBE)", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-28-native-hr", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Re-grounds _NYISO_OFFER_CURVE CC_REGULAR/CC_CHP/ST_GAS committed/econ_low/econ_high to NYISO's OWN CAMPD incremental-HR medians (derive_campd_marginal_hr.py, NY+NJ CEMS pooled 2023-25), removing the ERCOT-borrowed CC reach (1.21/1.24) and ST_GAS shape (1.10/1.45). Merit order preserved (CC econ_high eff HR 7.2/7.7 < ST committed 8.7). Result: the steam-side re-level nearly halves the 2024 ST_GAS under-run (-4.14->-2.22 TWh) and confirms steam was over-priced, but the bare CEMS marginal HR omits the competitive offer MARKUP (cost recovery + inframarginal rent) that NYISO has no offer disclosure to measure, cratering C3a mean LMP to -24.0%/-26.5%/-23.5% across 2023-25. Rule #1: a run missing real structure (the markup) is not a keeper. Carry-forward: ground a NYISO markup ON TOP of the native marginal HR (run 29).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-28-native-hr.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-29-synch-reserve", "label": "nyiso 29 synch-reserve [probe]", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-29-synch-reserve", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Path A of the downstate synchronised-reserve fix: a NYISO-only default-off lever adding a NYC 10-min SPINNING sub-requirement (0.5 x NYC 10-min total = 250 MW, the published 1/2-largest-contingency rule) supplied by an ONLINE-bounded reserve class (R <= sum online P), forcing idle NYC peakers to commit. Confirms the mechanism (CT_PEAKER 2023 -1.30->-0.92, 2024 -1.51->-1.05 TWh; CC over-run down) but INSUFFICIENT for the tail (C3c >$300 unchanged 1/0/8; C3a 2024 -9.7%->-11.0%) -- the grounded 250 MW spin clears cheaply and never fires the RCPF, exactly PR #877's prediction. Scopes path B (zonal/family commitment-aware reserve). Lever reverted on the branch; implementation preserved in the bundle model_changes.diff.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-29-synch-reserve.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-30-fwd-band", "label": "nyiso 30 fwd band", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-30-fwd-band", "definition": "NYISO import reconciliation made forecast-aware: backcast still targets the measured EIA-930 schedule (this run); forecast targets the neighbor's forecast net position (nyiso_forward_net_import_twh) or relaxes to the priced seam. Backcast path unchanged vs nyiso-27-cc-offer; re-solve confirms metrics hold.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-30-fwd-band.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-nyiso-31-synch-commit", "label": "nyiso 31 synch-commit [probe]", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "nyiso-31-synch-commit", "definition": "REJECTED PROBE (keeper stays nyiso-27 cc-offer). Path B of the downstate synchronised-reserve fix: commitment-gated spinning reserve (MILP-free). The NYC 250 MW spinning family rides the ordinary class-1 (quick-start) headroom while the P2 commitment screen zeroes decommitted availability, so the row equals Sum_online(pmax - P) -- the physically-correct synchronised headroom -- and a new reserve_adequacy_commit force-commits NYC quick-start to the measured 250 MW requirement. RESULT: a true no-op -- every scored metric reproduces the keeper essentially exactly (C1 CT_PEAKER 2023 -1.30/2024 -1.51 TWh; C3c >$300 1/0/8 vs actual 10/12/42; C3a 2024 -9.7%), and P1==P2 prices. ROOT CAUSE: the committed NYC quick-start fleet holds ~1,700 MW of genuine online headroom in the tightest hours vs a 500 MW 10-min / 250 MW spin requirement, so the measured reserve requirement is NON-BINDING and no RCPF tail can form -- commitment removes only offline pmax, not the dominant committed-but-backed-off headroom. Both path A (nyiso-29 online proxy) and path B are thus confirmed insufficient with grounded inputs; the >$300 downstate tail stays an accepted, ledgered open frontier on nyiso-27 (closing it would need an ungrounded requirement/adder, forbidden by rule #12). Wiring kept as a NYISO-only default-off scaffold; implementation in the bundle model_changes.diff.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-nyiso-31-synch-commit.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run155a-netload-gas-storage", "label": "run155a netload gas storage", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run155a-netload-gas-storage", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2024; ecrs-requirement=False; battery-adder=10.0", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run155a-netload-gas-storage.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run155b-netload-gas-adder0", "label": "run155b netload gas adder0", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run155b-netload-gas-adder0", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2025; ecrs-requirement=False; battery-adder=0.0", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run155b-netload-gas-adder0.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run155c-netload-gas-storage", "label": "run155c netload gas storage", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run155c-netload-gas-storage", "definition": "run134 recipe re-solved with measured 2023 AS; load-RRS-from-year=2023; storage-AS-from-year=2024; ecrs-requirement=False; battery-adder=0.0", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run155c-netload-gas-storage.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run156-ecrs-requirement-probe", "label": "run156 ecrs requirement probe", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run156-ecrs-requirement-probe", "definition": "(PROBE \u2014 REJECTED) run155a keeper + measured ECRS reserve requirement ON from 2023 (ercot_ecrs_requirement). Tested the May-2024 under-price hypothesis. Verdict: rejected. The flat +~2 GW ECRS reserve add does NOT fix May 2024 (load-wtd $23.6->$24.0, target $38-45) because May is not physically reserve-thin in the model \u2014 the co-opt ORDC steps only bite in genuinely-thin hours, so the add OVERSHOOTS Aug 2024 ($30.4->$53.5 vs actual 36) and catastrophically over-fires 2023-H2 (annual $48->$128, Aug $147->$598; no storage-AS credit in 2023). Battery throughput held (2024 0.78 TWh). Confirms the May miss is DAM ancillary-service co-optimized scarcity (hypothesis 4), not RT energy/reserve thinness. Keeper remains run154/run155a.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run156-ecrs-requirement-probe.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run157-dam-as-overlay", "label": "run157 dam as overlay", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run157-dam-as-overlay", "definition": "run155a keeper + NEW measured Day-Ahead AS-scarcity overlay (--ercot-dam-as-overlay): post-solve additive system-price adder = the binding DAM AS MCPC (per-hour max of cleared RegUp/RRS/ECRS/NonSpin, ERCOT 60-Day DAM Disclosure) on hours the DAM co-optimized energy+AS INTO scarcity (>$150). Fixes the May-2024 DAM-AS-co-opt under-price: May load-wtd $23.6->$46.7 (actual DA $44.8), 2024 monthly price-shape NRMSE 0.430->0.206. Held: Apr $26.4, Aug $34.7 (<$40), 2023 (Aug $147, scoped off via from-year=2024 so RTORDPA carries 2023 \u2014 no double-count), 2025 $34.8; battery 2024 0.775 TWh unchanged (post-solve, dispatch identical to run155a). Measured/backcast-able, not fit to LMP.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run157-dam-as-overlay.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run158-netload-gas-endogenous", "label": "run158 netload gas endogenous", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run158-netload-gas-endogenous", "definition": "(PROBE) the CURRENT keeper recipe (run157: DAM-AS overlay + RTORDPA, storage-AS-from-2024, STGAS-drag off, West net-load Waha gas step) re-solved with ONE change \u2014 the two-regime split frequency made ENDOGENOUS (gap G6 closed): collapse_freq from forecast West/Panhandle oversupply (West+Panhandle wind+solar > local load + WESTEX/PNHNDL export TTC) via fuel.ercot_west_oversupply_collapse_freq, replacing the measured Waha neg_day_freq. Validation (endogenous vs measured): 0.017/0.003/0.006 vs 0.030/0.420/0.110 for 2023/24/25 \u2014 the proxy tracks 2023 but is ~0 in 2024/25 because local load + the 12.68 GW takeaway absorbs all West VRE; the measured 2024 0.42 was a Permian gas-pipeline-takeaway event (no forward analogue, #12). Effect kept per #1/#11: West gas firm ~99% of hours, so CT_PEAKER under-runs (5.90/5.22/5.00 vs 7.56/8.21/7.11). NOT-YET (fuelmix 2024 ST_GAS, sysvol 2025 gas, price_mean/price_shape \u2014 run157-recipe + energy-only-dual limits). Demonstrates G6 closure on the keeper recipe; not a keeper.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run158-netload-gas-endogenous.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-25-run160-hsl-endogenous-curtailment", "label": "run160 hsl endogenous curtailment", "date": "2026-06-25", "shorthand": "run160-hsl-endogenous-curtailment", "definition": "run157 keeper config (verified-faithful reproduction; new current-code flags ercot_multiproduct_as_coopt/hydro_forecast_budget are default-off no-ops) + NEW uncurtailed-VRE wiring for the no-HSL years (renewables.py _UNCURTAILED_FALLBACK_ISOS). ERCOT 2024/25 have no NP6 HSL parquet, so they previously fell back to EIA-930 net-of-curtailment delivered generation (curtailment baked in, LP could not re-curtail). Now the dispatch gets an uncurtailed potential: the weather-year delivered profile grossed up by the per-tech reference curtailment rate from the most recent HSL year (ERCOT 2023: wind 4.67%, solar 6.29%), so the LP curtails endogenously. 2023 is unchanged (uses its own HSL). Modeled re-curtailment: wind 2.2-2.4%, solar 0.4-1.7% across 2023-25 vs measured/reference 4.67%/6.29% \u2014 the reduced 6-zone network curtails less than ERCOT's local transmission constraints (a DIAGNOSTIC, never a fit target, rule #11). Supersedes the mis-numbered run158-hsl (collided with another session's run158). NOT-YET: governance gate (no attestation) + C1 CC_REGULAR over-run. NOTE: the C1 regression vs run157 is dominated by INTERVENING main-code changes since run157 was solved, not this HSL change \u2014 the 2023 control year (renewables byte-identical to run157) independently regressed CC_REGULAR +8.57 TWh / COAL_PRB -3.78 / ST_GAS -5.10; the HSL change adds only ~+1.5 CC / ~-4 COAL_PRB to 2024 on top of that. Pre-existing ERCOT CC-over-run on main to investigate separately.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-25-run160-hsl-endogenous-curtailment.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-160-as-aware-commit", "label": "160 as aware commit", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "160-as-aware-commit", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 160.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-160-as-aware-commit.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-161-reserve-supply-cap", "label": "161 reserve supply cap", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "161-reserve-supply-cap", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle 161.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "ERCOT", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-161-reserve-supply-cap.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-caiso-30-ct-baseline", "label": "caiso 30 ct baseline", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "caiso-30-ct-baseline", "definition": "CAISO 30: add the year-round local-RA BASELINE to the CT_PEAKER reliability floor (caiso_ct_floor_base=0.049 = measured median CAMPD CT_PEAKER evening (15-22) capacity factor on cool days TMAX<25C, 2023-2025 \u2014 the regression intercept the temperature hot-limb fit clips to zero). frac = clip(base + slope*(TMAX-T0), base, cap). Lifts CT_PEAKER toward its measured local-RA level and trims CC_REGULAR with no price-level change. Strict improvement over caiso-29; CT_CHP EOR over-dispatch remains the open C1 blocker (gas-floor/CHP coupling, documented in docs/caiso-ct-reliability-floor-2026-06.md).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-caiso-30-ct-baseline.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-caiso-31-eor-power", "label": "caiso 31 eor power", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "caiso-31-eor-power", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle caiso31_eor.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-caiso-31-eor-power.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-caiso-32-evening-export", "label": "caiso 32 evening export", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "caiso-32-evening-export", "definition": "Calibration run from bundle caiso32_eor_exportcap.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "CAISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-caiso-32-evening-export.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-miso13-manitoba-firm-per", "label": "miso13 manitoba firm per-year", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "miso13-manitoba-firm-per", "definition": "miso13 manitoba-firm-peryear: miso12 (seam-deliverability) PLUS one structural lever \u2014 replace the FLAT Manitoba Hydro firm-import block (1400 MW must-flow, ~12.3 TWh/yr contract midpoint) with the MEASURED per-year firm-hydro DELIVERY (backcast-only): MISO_MANITOBA_FIRM_IMPORT_MW_BY_YEAR = 726/531/224 MW (6.36/4.65/1.96 TWh, 2023/24/25), the GROSS directed MHEB import from the EIA-930 BA-to-BA interchange parquet (-min(mw,0) hours), annualized flat \u2014 capturing Manitoba's 2024-25 drought collapse. A physical hydro-availability/firm-contract quantity (rules #11/#12: pre-LP, forward-regenerable, drought-responsive, NOT a net-MWh residual fit; gross firm delivery, not net interchange), same class as the nuclear refuel derate + seam envelope; forecast keeps the flat 1400 MW (byte-identical). Gated by mode+year, MISO-only, no new flag; derive/recheck via scripts/derive_manitoba_firm_import.py --check. RESULT (net interchange, EIA +=export): 2023 -47.9\u2192-43.9 (actual -37.9, over-import shrinks); 2024 -25.1\u2192-19.8 (actual -23.1) with COMPOSITION fixed \u2014 Manitoba seam +12.3\u2192+4.65 (measured +3.0) and the freed ~7.6 TWh lifts MISO's LMP so PJM imports rise +8.4\u2192+10.5 toward measured +32.2 (predicted reinforcement). The faithful cap also UNMASKS the dominant remaining miss: dear-gas 2025 flips to net export +14.4 (actual -19.0) because removing the ~10 TWh phantom Manitoba import exposes the COAL OVER-RUN (coal 239.7 vs 201.0 EIA-923 +38.6; gas already near-exact 211.4 vs 207.6) = the separate next lever (dear-gas coal offer / PRB passthrough), NOT an import-level error. KEEPER on rule-#11/#1 faithfulness (accurate measured firm delivery replaces a flat estimate that was silently compensating for the coal length); determination NOT-YET overall with the misses left as honest FAILs (2025 coal, South-seam under-export, SPP/PJM neighbor price, no scarcity tail). Solved 2023+2024+2025 in one invocation (per-year RSS ~10 GB, released between years).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "MISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-miso13-manitoba-firm-per.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-nyiso-32-steam-markup", "label": "nyiso 32 steam-markup [keeper]", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "nyiso-32-steam-markup", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes nyiso-27 cc-offer). The documented run-29 carry-forward from the rejected nyiso-28 native-hr probe: re-level the legacy gas-steam (ST_GAS) offer from the ERCOT-shaped rising ramp (committed 0.97 / econ_low 1.10 / econ_high 1.45) to NYISO's OWN measured CAMPD marginal heat rate x a grounded competitive markup (committed 1.05 / econ_low 1.08 / econ_high 1.13). Markup = the CC class's own defensible reach ratio (keeper CC econ_high 1.21 / native CC marginal 0.925 = 1.31x) applied to the flat steam native marginal HR (~0.82-0.83); CC reach UNCHANGED at the nyiso-27 keeper (the markup that holds the clearing price). Merit order preserved (steam eff HR 11.1-12.0 > CC 9.4, < CT 16.1; no inversion). Result -- HARD C1 fuel-mix IMPROVES across the board: 2023 ST_GAS -1.26 -> -0.01 TWh (now PASS, the flat measured band reproduces measured steam volume almost EXACTLY -> offer level validated a priori, not residual-fitted), CC_REGULAR +1.70 -> +0.99 (PASS); 2024 ST_GAS -4.14 -> -3.28, CC_REGULAR +2.09 -> +1.74. dispatch_corr stays PASS (gas r=0.91/0.84/0.80). SOFT C3a mean LMP regresses to a documented CAVEAT (2023 in-band -> -10.5%, 2024 -9.7% -> -11.2%): the keeper-27 steep steam ramp was a COMPENSATING over-pricing that propped up the mid-merit price; removing it (the measured-grounded move) exposes the real root cause -- the missing reserve-scarcity / RCPF tail (same cause as the CT_PEAKER C1 miss), confirmed NON-CLOSABLE with grounded inputs by nyiso-29 (online-proxy spin) and nyiso-31 (commitment-gated spin). Kept per CLAUDE.md rule #1 (a measured mechanism stays in even though the fit worsens; reverting would reach C3a through a mechanism that isn't real and re-open the volume miss; raising steam to chase C3a would distort the volume-validated level, rule #12) -- the same promotion principle as nyiso-25. Import/export: net-interchange import reconciliation + priced node unchanged; the downstate interface-TTC congestion separation (U7) stays data-blocked. Governance C6 PASS; determination NOT-YET (price_shape/price_tail = the genuine reserve-scarcity model gap, unledgered MODEL MISS, same open frontier on every NYISO keeper). Reproduce: the nyiso-27 keeper flags + --offer-curve-json '{\"ST_GAS\": {\"committed\": 1.05, \"econ_low\": 1.08, \"econ_high\": 1.13}}'.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-nyiso-32-steam-markup.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-26-pjm-51-seam-se", "label": "pjm 51 seam se", "date": "2026-06-26", "shorthand": "pjm-51-seam-se", "definition": "(DIAGNOSTIC) pjm-49/50 seam base (pjm-48 coal keeper + MISO/NYISO hr_by_year + firm-export floor + losses hurdle + TVA/LGEE) + the one new lever: Southeast (Carolinas/TVA/LGEE) gas-INELASTIC affine reference price (_HR_GAS_ELASTIC 5.6*gas+14.2, OLS-fit to documented Into-Southern/SERC hub levels, BLIND to flow; derive_southeast_inelastic_hr.py). RESULT -- the named root cause is FIXED: the 2025 wrong-direction Southeast export flips back to correct IMPORT (Carolinas/TVA/LGEE per-seam +2.0/+1.3/+0.9 wrong at pjm-50 -> -4.4/-3.3/-2.5 vs per-tie -5.9/-5.4/-2.3). Old flat HR 11.6 priced SE at $40.8 in dear-gas 2025 (~$2 below PJM, diurnal swing flipped it export); the affine holds it at $33.9 (~$9 below PJM), so the structural import direction holds across the gas cycle. BUT the fix UNMASKS the dominant remaining error and UNIFIES it with the coal/gas class miss: correctly importing cheap SE power lowers PJM's modeled price, so PJM out-competes MISO/NYISO and over-exports MORE (2025 MISO +37.8 vs pjm-50 +20.0; net-interchange MAE vs EIA-930 rises to 23.5 from pjm-50's 15.2). The flat-SE was MASKING this by bleeding excess off as spurious SE exports. The MISO/NYISO seams already carry convex self-limiting (load_shape_exponent 1.6) yet still saturate at the cap -- because the over-export is NOT a seam-pricing bug but PJM being structurally UNDER-priced: it over-generates fossil (gas +73 TWh 2025, coal classes off) which makes it cheaper than its neighbors. So 'import/export' and 'class missed' are the SAME root cause -- PJM's fleet is too cheap. The SE gas-inelastic pricing is structurally correct and stays in (rule #1) even though net worsens; pjm-48 remains the PJM keeper. Next: the coal/gas offer-curve retune (the keeper's separate model-side workstream) is the lever for BOTH; a Southeast FIRM-IMPORT floor (mirror of the firm-export floor) recovers the 2024 SE import the affine price correctly stops faking. Years 2023-25, single-process sequential solve.", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "PJM", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-26-pjm-51-seam-se.js"}, {"id": "2026-06-27-nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor", "label": "nyiso 33 ct-tempfloor [keeper]", "date": "2026-06-27", "shorthand": "nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor", "definition": "NEW KEEPER (supersedes nyiso-32 steam-markup). Adds a temperature-keyed DOWNSTATE CT_PEAKER local-reliability floor (--nyiso-ct-reliability-floor) on top of the nyiso-32 steam-markup keeper -- the CAISO local-RA CT floor ported to NYISO's cable-constrained downstate load pockets (NYC zone J, Long Island zone K, Lower Hudson). On hot afternoons the downstate cooling load climbs and the UPNY-SENY / LI-cable import limits bind, so fast-start GTs are held online for local capacity-area reliability; an energy-only LP imports cheap upstate/NYC CC instead and under-runs CT_PEAKER. Floor = clip(base + slope*(TMAX-25), base, cap) x available downstate-CT capacity over HB14-21, with slope=0.053/degC, cap=0.68, base=0.13 -- ALL regressed a priori from the measured downstate CAMPD CT_PEAKER evening CF vs NYC daily max temperature (NOAA GHCN), pooled 2023-2025 (scripts/derive_nyiso_ct_reliability_floor.py; archived data/raw/nyiso-weather/). A physical heat->commitment rule, forward-reproducible (a forecast year pins a weather year, hence a TMAX series), NOT a CEMS pin and NOT residual-tuned (CT_PEAKER lands UNDER actual, so not over-forced). Result -- the HARD C1 fuel-mix improves decisively: CT_PEAKER (the open frontier on EVERY prior NYISO keeper, and confirmed non-closable via a grounded reserve REQUIREMENT by nyiso-29/31) 2023 -1.73 -> -0.85 TWh and 2024 -1.92 -> -0.85 TWh, FAIL -> PASS both scored years; the complementary CC_REGULAR over-run drops 2023 +0.99 -> +0.63 (PASS) and 2024 +1.74 -> +1.25 (FAIL -> PASS). 2023 now has every gas class in C1 tolerance. dispatch_corr stays PASS (gas r=0.91/0.85/0.80). TRADEOFF (kept per rule #1): C3a mean LMP regresses ~1.3pp (2023 -10.5 -> -11.7%, 2024 -11.2 -> -12.7%, 2025 -7.3 -> -8.6%) because the floor is a min-gen (must-run) representation -- it recovers the local-RA ENERGY but adds inframarginal supply rather than letting the peakers SET a scarcity price; the price side (C3a/C3c) remains the ledgered reserve-scarcity frontier (an ORDC/reserve-scarcity demand curve, non-closable with grounded inputs per nyiso-29/31). Remaining C1 miss: 2024 ST_GAS -3.77 TWh (the floor pulls some evening energy from downstate steam too). Most structurally faithful NYISO config to date: it adds a real, measured, forward-reproducible local-reliability mechanism that closes the largest C1 miss -- the rule-#1 'right market structure' step the reserve-requirement attempts could not take. Governance C6 PASS; determination NOT-YET (2024 ST_GAS C1 + price_shape/price_tail reserve-scarcity gap). Reproduce: nyiso-32 keeper flags + --nyiso-ct-reliability-floor (now the live default for NYISO).", "years": [2023, 2024, 2025], "iso": "NYISO", "file": "frontend/data/backcast/runs/2026-06-27-nyiso-33-ct-tempfloor.js"}];